WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.366 --> 00:00.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:00.636 --> 00:01.006 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:02.896 --> 00:05.856 align:middle
The pandemic continues
to cause tremendous human

00:05.856 --> 00:07.276 align:middle
and economic hardship here

00:07.276 --> 00:09.126 align:middle
in the United States
and around the world.

00:10.466 --> 00:11.636 align:middle
The most important response

00:11.636 --> 00:13.466 align:middle
to this public health
crisis has come

00:13.466 --> 00:16.366 align:middle
from our health-care workers,
and we remain grateful to them

00:16.836 --> 00:19.496 align:middle
and to the many other essential
workers for putting themselves

00:19.496 --> 00:23.326 align:middle
at risk day after day in service
to others and to our country.

00:24.606 --> 00:26.226 align:middle
All of us have a role to play

00:26.226 --> 00:28.216 align:middle
in our nation's response
to the pandemic.

00:28.216 --> 00:31.636 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we remain committed

00:31.636 --> 00:34.636 align:middle
to using our tools to do
what we can, and for as long

00:34.636 --> 00:37.596 align:middle
as it takes, to provide
some relief and stability,

00:38.356 --> 00:41.116 align:middle
to ensure that the recovery
will be as strong as possible,

00:41.746 --> 00:44.206 align:middle
and to limit lasting
damage to the economy.

00:44.756 --> 00:48.366 align:middle
In recent months,
economic activity picked

00:48.366 --> 00:50.806 align:middle
up as the economy
began to reopen.

00:51.896 --> 00:53.726 align:middle
Many businesses opened
their doors,

00:54.006 --> 00:57.586 align:middle
factories restarted production,
and more people left their homes

00:57.586 --> 00:59.266 align:middle
to engage in various activities.

00:59.266 --> 01:03.706 align:middle
As a result, household
spending looks to have recovered

01:03.706 --> 01:06.606 align:middle
about half of its earlier
decline, although spending

01:06.606 --> 01:08.466 align:middle
for services such as air travel

01:08.516 --> 01:11.466 align:middle
and hotels has shown
much less of a pickup.

01:12.856 --> 01:15.446 align:middle
The recovery in household
spending also likely owes

01:15.446 --> 01:18.836 align:middle
to federal stimulus payments and
expanded unemployment benefits,

01:19.386 --> 01:20.806 align:middle
which provided substantial

01:20.806 --> 01:23.016 align:middle
and timely support
to household incomes.

01:23.936 --> 01:28.506 align:middle
In contrast, indicators of
business fixed investment have

01:28.506 --> 01:29.656 align:middle
yet to show a recovery.

01:30.706 --> 01:33.686 align:middle
Even with the improved
economic news in May and June,

01:33.686 --> 01:36.686 align:middle
overall activity remains well
below its level before the

01:36.686 --> 01:39.906 align:middle
pandemic, and the
contraction in real GDP

01:39.906 --> 01:43.556 align:middle
in the second quarter will
likely be the largest on record.

01:44.296 --> 01:46.896 align:middle
The labor market has
followed a similar pattern.

01:47.306 --> 01:49.616 align:middle
After precipitous drops
in March and April,

01:50.086 --> 01:53.576 align:middle
employment rose strongly in May
and June as many people returned

01:53.576 --> 01:55.426 align:middle
to work from temporary layoffs.

01:56.406 --> 01:59.666 align:middle
As a result, of the roughly 22
million jobs that had been lost,

02:00.226 --> 02:02.056 align:middle
about one-third had
been regained

02:02.056 --> 02:03.656 align:middle
as of the June payroll report.

02:04.566 --> 02:07.446 align:middle
The unemployment rate
declined in May and June but,

02:07.446 --> 02:10.136 align:middle
at 11.1 percent, remains far

02:10.136 --> 02:12.966 align:middle
above its level before
the outbreak and greater

02:13.046 --> 02:15.436 align:middle
than the peak during the
Global Financial Crisis.

02:15.956 --> 02:19.586 align:middle
In addition, the downturn
has not fallen equally

02:19.586 --> 02:21.896 align:middle
on all Americans,
and those least able

02:21.896 --> 02:23.716 align:middle
to bear the burden have
been the most affected.

02:24.606 --> 02:25.856 align:middle
In particular, the rise

02:25.856 --> 02:27.846 align:middle
in joblessness has
been especially severe

02:27.846 --> 02:30.266 align:middle
for lower-wage workers,
for women,

02:30.356 --> 02:33.606 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

02:34.256 --> 02:37.516 align:middle
This reversal of economic
fortune has upended many lives

02:37.946 --> 02:40.086 align:middle
and created great
uncertainty about the future.

02:40.616 --> 02:44.056 align:middle
The pandemic has also
left a significant imprint

02:44.056 --> 02:44.766 align:middle
on inflation.

02:45.866 --> 02:49.006 align:middle
For some goods, including food,
supply constraints have led

02:49.006 --> 02:51.666 align:middle
to notably higher prices,
adding to the burden

02:51.816 --> 02:53.576 align:middle
for those struggling
with lost income.

02:54.556 --> 02:56.256 align:middle
More broadly, however,
weaker demand,

02:56.256 --> 02:58.306 align:middle
especially in sectors-such
as travel

02:58.306 --> 03:01.336 align:middle
and hospitality-that have been
most affected by the pandemic,

03:01.756 --> 03:04.266 align:middle
has held down consumer
prices, and, overall,

03:04.266 --> 03:06.896 align:middle
inflation is running well
below our symmetric 2

03:06.896 --> 03:07.886 align:middle
percent objective.

03:08.436 --> 03:11.446 align:middle
Along with the recent increases

03:11.446 --> 03:13.986 align:middle
in economic activity
have come new challenges.

03:14.796 --> 03:18.076 align:middle
After declining gradually from
a peak near the end of April,

03:18.256 --> 03:22.346 align:middle
the number of COVID-19 cases has
increased sharply in many parts

03:22.346 --> 03:24.336 align:middle
of the country since mid-June.

03:25.016 --> 03:28.116 align:middle
We have thus entered a new
phase in containing the virus,

03:28.206 --> 03:31.746 align:middle
which is essential to protect
both our health and our economy.

03:33.236 --> 03:37.846 align:middle
As we have emphasized throughout
the pandemic, the path forward

03:37.846 --> 03:40.196 align:middle
for the economy is
extraordinarily uncertain

03:40.706 --> 03:43.246 align:middle
and will depend in large
part on our success

03:43.246 --> 03:44.836 align:middle
in keeping the virus in check.

03:45.796 --> 03:48.296 align:middle
Indeed, we have seen some
signs in recent weeks

03:48.446 --> 03:51.286 align:middle
that the increase in virus
cases and the renewed measures

03:51.286 --> 03:54.486 align:middle
to control it are starting to
weigh on economic activity.

03:55.226 --> 03:57.936 align:middle
For example, some measures
of consumer spending based

03:57.936 --> 04:00.686 align:middle
on debit card and credit
card use have moved

04:00.686 --> 04:01.956 align:middle
down since late June,

04:02.796 --> 04:05.586 align:middle
while recent labor market
indicators point to a slowing

04:05.586 --> 04:08.406 align:middle
in job growth, especially
among smaller businesses.

04:09.726 --> 04:12.786 align:middle
A full recovery is unlikely
until people are confident

04:12.946 --> 04:15.996 align:middle
that it's safe to reengage in
a broad range of activities.

04:17.316 --> 04:20.926 align:middle
The path forward will also
depend on policy actions taken

04:20.926 --> 04:23.126 align:middle
at all levels of
government to provide relief

04:23.586 --> 04:25.936 align:middle
and to support the recovery
for as long as needed.

04:26.536 --> 04:30.286 align:middle
The Federal Reserve's response
to this crisis has been guided

04:30.286 --> 04:32.936 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:32.936 --> 04:35.046 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people,

04:35.616 --> 04:38.196 align:middle
along with our responsibilities
to promote the stability

04:38.196 --> 04:39.226 align:middle
of the financial system.

04:39.836 --> 04:42.956 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

04:42.956 --> 04:45.176 align:middle
to support the economy
in this challenging time.

04:46.366 --> 04:49.446 align:middle
We have held our policy rate
near zero since mid-March

04:49.446 --> 04:51.006 align:middle
and have stated that
we will keep it there

04:51.336 --> 04:54.406 align:middle
until we are confident that the
economy has weathered recent

04:54.406 --> 04:57.926 align:middle
events and is on track to
achieve our maximum-employment

04:57.926 --> 04:59.366 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

05:00.736 --> 05:03.236 align:middle
We've been purchasing
sizable quantities of Treasury

05:03.236 --> 05:05.626 align:middle
and agency mortgage-backed
securities in order

05:05.626 --> 05:08.856 align:middle
to support orderly conditions
in the markets, which are vital

05:08.856 --> 05:10.506 align:middle
to the flow of credit
in the economy.

05:11.686 --> 05:13.736 align:middle
To sustain smooth
market functioning

05:13.736 --> 05:16.586 align:middle
and foster effective
transmission of monetary policy

05:16.586 --> 05:19.136 align:middle
to broader financial
conditions, we will continue

05:19.136 --> 05:21.196 align:middle
to increase our holdings
of Treasury

05:21.196 --> 05:22.936 align:middle
and agency mortgage-backed
securities

05:23.246 --> 05:24.936 align:middle
at least at the current pace.

05:26.076 --> 05:29.106 align:middle
These purchases are also
fostering more accommodative

05:29.106 --> 05:30.096 align:middle
financial conditions.

05:31.426 --> 05:33.406 align:middle
The Federal Reserve has
also been taking broad

05:33.406 --> 05:36.136 align:middle
and forceful actions to more
directly support the flow

05:36.136 --> 05:39.566 align:middle
of credit in the economy for
households, for businesses large

05:39.566 --> 05:41.896 align:middle
and small, and for state
and local governments.

05:42.726 --> 05:44.966 align:middle
Without access to credit,
families could be forced

05:44.966 --> 05:47.616 align:middle
to cut back on necessities
or even lose their homes.

05:48.516 --> 05:51.336 align:middle
Businesses could be forced
to downsize or close,

05:51.366 --> 05:54.776 align:middle
resulting in further job
losses and income losses

05:54.776 --> 05:56.106 align:middle
and worsening the downturn.

05:57.136 --> 05:59.416 align:middle
Preserving the flow of
credit is thus essential

05:59.416 --> 06:01.436 align:middle
for mitigating the
damage to the economy

06:01.856 --> 06:03.076 align:middle
and promoting the recovery.

06:04.926 --> 06:07.966 align:middle
Many of our programs rely
on emergency lending powers

06:07.966 --> 06:10.086 align:middle
that require the support
of the Treasury Department

06:10.416 --> 06:13.676 align:middle
and are available only in
very unusual circumstances,

06:14.046 --> 06:16.146 align:middle
such as those we find
ourselves in today.

06:17.166 --> 06:20.446 align:middle
These programs benefit the
economy by providing financing

06:20.446 --> 06:22.136 align:middle
where it is not otherwise
available.

06:22.916 --> 06:26.126 align:middle
In addition, by serving as a
backstop to key credit markets,

06:26.406 --> 06:29.026 align:middle
the programs appear to have
significantly increased the

06:29.026 --> 06:32.206 align:middle
extension of credit
from private lenders.

06:32.646 --> 06:35.896 align:middle
We are deploying
these lending powers

06:35.896 --> 06:38.816 align:middle
to an unprecedented extent,
enabled in large part

06:38.896 --> 06:42.066 align:middle
by financial backing and support
from Congress and the Treasury.

06:42.726 --> 06:45.646 align:middle
We will continue to use these
powers until we are confident

06:45.646 --> 06:47.576 align:middle
that we are solidly on
the road to recovery.

06:48.646 --> 06:50.506 align:middle
This week we extended
these programs

06:50.506 --> 06:51.496 align:middle
through the end of the year.

06:51.796 --> 06:55.676 align:middle
When the time comes, after
the crisis has passed,

06:56.146 --> 06:59.066 align:middle
we will put these emergency
tools back in the toolbox.

07:00.176 --> 07:02.076 align:middle
As I have emphasized before,

07:02.306 --> 07:04.776 align:middle
these are lending powers,
not spending powers.

07:05.496 --> 07:08.456 align:middle
The Fed cannot grant money
to particular beneficiaries.

07:08.946 --> 07:11.166 align:middle
We can only create
programs or facilities

07:11.166 --> 07:15.306 align:middle
with broad-based eligibility to
make loans to solvent entities

07:15.416 --> 07:18.126 align:middle
with the expectation that
the loans will be repaid.

07:19.276 --> 07:21.556 align:middle
Many borrowers will
benefit from these programs,

07:21.556 --> 07:22.966 align:middle
as will the overall economy.

07:23.896 --> 07:26.606 align:middle
But for many others, getting
a loan that may be difficult

07:26.606 --> 07:28.356 align:middle
to repay may not be the answer.

07:29.096 --> 07:31.876 align:middle
In these cases, direct
fiscal support may be needed.

07:32.966 --> 07:35.426 align:middle
Elected officials have
the power to tax and spend

07:35.426 --> 07:37.906 align:middle
and to make decisions about
where we, as a society,

07:37.906 --> 07:39.916 align:middle
should direct our
collective resources.

07:40.886 --> 07:42.096 align:middle
The fiscal policy actions

07:42.096 --> 07:44.606 align:middle
that have been taken thus far
have made a critical difference

07:44.606 --> 07:47.856 align:middle
to families, businesses, and
communities across the country.

07:48.766 --> 07:52.016 align:middle
Even so, the current economic
downturn is the most severe

07:52.016 --> 07:52.976 align:middle
in our lifetimes.

07:53.416 --> 07:55.676 align:middle
It will take a while to
get back to the levels

07:55.676 --> 07:58.096 align:middle
of economic activity and
employment that prevailed

07:58.506 --> 07:59.606 align:middle
at the beginning of the year,

08:00.016 --> 08:02.986 align:middle
and it will take continued
support from both monetary

08:02.986 --> 08:05.676 align:middle
and fiscal policy
to achieve that.

08:07.216 --> 08:09.676 align:middle
Before taking your questions,
I'll provide an update

08:10.076 --> 08:12.686 align:middle
on our review of our
monetary policy framework.

08:12.796 --> 08:13.326 align:middle
[Clears throat] Excuse me.

08:14.056 --> 08:16.026 align:middle
As a reminder, we began
this public review

08:16.026 --> 08:19.126 align:middle
of our monetary policy
strategy, tools,

08:19.126 --> 08:21.346 align:middle
and communication
practices-a first

08:21.346 --> 08:23.316 align:middle
for the Federal Reserve-early
last year.

08:23.986 --> 08:26.446 align:middle
Our purpose has been to
take a comprehensive look

08:26.446 --> 08:29.176 align:middle
at how we can best meet
our maximum-employment

08:29.176 --> 08:33.236 align:middle
and price-stability objectives
in the years ahead, particularly

08:33.236 --> 08:34.916 align:middle
in light of the generally
lower level

08:34.916 --> 08:36.346 align:middle
of interest rates
around the world.

08:37.316 --> 08:40.026 align:middle
As is evident in our current
situation, the lower level

08:40.026 --> 08:43.096 align:middle
of interest rates has reduced
the scope for the Committee

08:43.096 --> 08:45.636 align:middle
to support the economy by
cutting interest rates.

08:46.536 --> 08:49.066 align:middle
Our plans to conclude
this review were,

08:49.066 --> 08:51.506 align:middle
like so many things,
delayed by the pandemic.

08:52.446 --> 08:55.236 align:middle
At this meeting, my colleagues
and I resumed our discussions.

08:55.666 --> 08:59.736 align:middle
Our focus was on possible
enhancements to our Statement

08:59.736 --> 09:02.636 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and
Monetary Policy Strategy.

09:03.606 --> 09:06.976 align:middle
This document states our
goals, articulates our approach

09:06.976 --> 09:08.696 align:middle
to monetary policy, and serves

09:08.696 --> 09:10.606 align:middle
at the foundation-as
the foundation

09:10.606 --> 09:11.836 align:middle
for our policy actions.

09:12.856 --> 09:15.566 align:middle
While I do not have any
details to share with you today,

09:15.756 --> 09:18.266 align:middle
I am confident that we will
continue to make progress

09:18.756 --> 09:19.326 align:middle
and will wrap

09:19.326 --> 09:21.576 align:middle
up our deliberations
in the near future.

09:22.116 --> 09:25.896 align:middle
We understand that the work of
the Fed touches communities,

09:26.036 --> 09:28.276 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

09:29.126 --> 09:31.826 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

09:32.606 --> 09:35.216 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

09:35.216 --> 09:38.186 align:middle
to support the economy and to
help assure that the recovery

09:38.186 --> 09:41.756 align:middle
from this difficult period
will be as robust as possible.

09:42.896 --> 09:43.316 align:middle
Thank you.

09:43.416 --> 09:45.106 align:middle
I will look forward
to your questions.

09:45.916 --> 09:46.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:47.596 --> 09:49.076 align:middle
James Politi.

09:49.076 --> 09:50.706 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

09:51.266 --> 09:55.686 align:middle
Hi there. James Politi
with the Financial Times.

09:55.686 --> 09:58.326 align:middle
Chair Powell, the
Fed today decided

09:58.326 --> 10:04.156 align:middle
to extend dollar liquidity
swap lines with a number

10:04.156 --> 10:05.736 align:middle
of central banks
around the world.

10:06.126 --> 10:09.606 align:middle
Why was that important
for the Fed?

10:09.656 --> 10:14.116 align:middle
And how concerned are you about
dollar shortages persisting

10:14.526 --> 10:15.816 align:middle
over time through the pandemic?

10:15.816 --> 10:17.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:17.396 --> 10:22.366 align:middle
Well, so our-our dollar swap
lines-we introduced those back

10:22.366 --> 10:25.086 align:middle
at the beginning
of-of this episode

10:25.086 --> 10:28.626 align:middle
after the pandemic
made itself present.

10:29.506 --> 10:31.626 align:middle
And dollar funding markets were

10:31.626 --> 10:33.366 align:middle
in very difficult
shape at the time.

10:33.496 --> 10:40.036 align:middle
And the introduction of the swap
lines has really restored dollar

10:40.036 --> 10:41.446 align:middle
funding markets around the world

10:42.056 --> 10:45.576 align:middle
to fairly normal
levels of activity.

10:45.576 --> 10:48.066 align:middle
And so they kind of
served their purpose.

10:48.596 --> 10:55.236 align:middle
But we extended them-I guess,
yesterday morning-really

10:55.236 --> 10:58.396 align:middle
to facilitate planning by
other central banks and just

10:58.396 --> 11:00.986 align:middle
so people will know that those
facilities are still there.

11:01.386 --> 11:04.146 align:middle
We want them to remain
in place and be available

11:04.146 --> 11:05.376 align:middle
as long as they are needed.

11:05.976 --> 11:07.556 align:middle
And since, you know, the crisis

11:07.556 --> 11:11.656 align:middle
and the economic fallout
from-from the pandemic are far

11:11.656 --> 11:13.626 align:middle
from over, we're
going to leave those

11:13.626 --> 11:15.756 align:middle
in place for the time being.

11:15.756 --> 11:17.756 align:middle
And we'll leave them in
place until we're confident

11:17.756 --> 11:19.006 align:middle
that they're no longer needed.

11:19.306 --> 11:21.656 align:middle
There's nothing that's going
on in the market right now

11:21.656 --> 11:22.936 align:middle
that raises any concerns.

11:22.936 --> 11:26.006 align:middle
It's just, we want them to be
there as a backstop for markets.

11:26.446 --> 11:29.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:29.906 --> 11:31.816 align:middle
Okay. Steve Liesman.

11:34.326 --> 11:35.386 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

11:35.386 --> 11:36.386 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

11:36.386 --> 11:37.686 align:middle
I hope that you can hear me.

11:38.286 --> 11:42.366 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, given-you've
said several times now

11:42.366 --> 11:44.256 align:middle
that you have lending,
not spending, powers.

11:44.356 --> 11:48.196 align:middle
Given that a lot of the capacity

11:48.196 --> 11:51.506 align:middle
of these programs has not been
used and, really, barely used,

11:51.956 --> 11:54.416 align:middle
do you think that some of this
money that has been dedicated

11:54.416 --> 11:57.256 align:middle
by the Treasury to the Federal
Reserve should be considered

11:57.256 --> 12:00.826 align:middle
to be used by Congress as direct
grants to businesses that are

12:00.826 --> 12:02.796 align:middle
in need right now
or to households,

12:02.796 --> 12:06.716 align:middle
given that Congress is now
saying that money is limited

12:06.836 --> 12:08.416 align:middle
and they shouldn't
take-they do not want

12:08.416 --> 12:11.016 align:middle
to pass a very large
stimulus bill?

12:11.016 --> 12:15.366 align:middle
Is this money doing the best
for the nation as a backstop

12:15.366 --> 12:17.706 align:middle
for Federal Reserve
programs that don't seem

12:17.706 --> 12:19.496 align:middle
to be being used all
that much right now?

12:19.586 --> 12:19.856 align:middle
Thank you.

12:19.856 --> 12:20.736 align:middle
And I have a follow-up, sir.

12:21.036 --> 12:21.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:21.556 --> 12:24.086 align:middle
So they-that-that's really
a question for Congress.

12:24.086 --> 12:27.766 align:middle
They-they appropriated that
money, and-$454 billion

12:27.766 --> 12:31.856 align:middle
for our facilities, and it's
really a question for them.

12:32.486 --> 12:35.666 align:middle
So you're right that our
facilities have not-we haven't

12:35.666 --> 12:36.996 align:middle
done as much lending
as we thought.

12:37.046 --> 12:38.516 align:middle
But, in very substantial
measure,

12:38.516 --> 12:42.936 align:middle
that's because markets started
working again fairly soon

12:42.936 --> 12:44.756 align:middle
after we announced the
facilities, particularly

12:44.756 --> 12:45.966 align:middle
in the corporate
credit facilities,

12:45.966 --> 12:48.106 align:middle
also in the muni-in
the muni space,

12:48.106 --> 12:51.896 align:middle
and also in the short-term
funding facilities-the three

12:51.896 --> 12:52.716 align:middle
of those that we set up.

12:52.716 --> 12:56.986 align:middle
So we didn't turn out to
need the kind of funding

12:56.986 --> 12:57.716 align:middle
that we thought we would.

12:58.066 --> 12:59.206 align:middle
On the other hand,
it's important

12:59.206 --> 13:00.796 align:middle
that the facilities
stay in place,

13:00.796 --> 13:03.836 align:middle
and that's why we
extended them yesterday.

13:03.896 --> 13:06.676 align:middle
It's important that they still
stay in place until we're,

13:07.206 --> 13:11.526 align:middle
you know, very confident that
the turmoil from the pandemic

13:11.526 --> 13:14.006 align:middle
and the economic
fallout are behind us.

13:14.466 --> 13:19.816 align:middle
So I-I can't really speak to
what Congress should do with it,

13:19.816 --> 13:21.676 align:middle
but it's important that
the facilities be there

13:21.676 --> 13:25.786 align:middle
and be fully funded in case the
needs do arise down the road.

13:25.786 --> 13:26.836 align:middle
We don't see them now, but,

13:26.836 --> 13:28.466 align:middle
in case they do arise,
they should be there.

13:28.466 --> 13:30.166 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:30.166 --> 13:31.736 align:middle
Has any consideration-thank you.

13:31.736 --> 13:34.806 align:middle
Has any consideration been
given to asking Congress

13:34.806 --> 13:37.296 align:middle
to allow the Federal Reserve to
lend to companies in bankruptcy?

13:37.666 --> 13:37.976 align:middle
Thank you.

13:37.976 --> 13:38.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:39.096 --> 13:40.036 align:middle
Not that I'm aware of, no.

13:42.886 --> 13:43.166 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:44.876 --> 13:45.466 align:middle
Thank you.

13:45.516 --> 13:46.236 align:middle
Nick Timiraos.

13:58.306 --> 13:58.696 align:middle
[No response] Nick,
are you there?

13:58.696 --> 13:59.816 align:middle
[No response] UNIDENTIFIED
SPEAKER.

14:19.366 --> 14:19.966 align:middle
[Aside] I didn't
hear you say that.

14:19.966 --> 14:20.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:24.656 --> 14:26.196 align:middle
We'll go to David Gura, NBC.

14:26.256 --> 14:26.886 align:middle
DAVID GURA.

14:27.566 --> 14:29.006 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, thank
you very much.

14:29.256 --> 14:31.016 align:middle
No doubt you were
paying attention when two

14:31.016 --> 14:31.936 align:middle
of your predecessors were

14:31.936 --> 14:34.156 align:middle
up on Capitol Hill just a
few days ago answering some

14:34.156 --> 14:35.606 align:middle
questions from-from lawmakers.

14:35.606 --> 14:37.736 align:middle
And a question they
each answered had to do

14:37.736 --> 14:40.476 align:middle
with the continuation of these
pandemic unemployment benefits.

14:40.476 --> 14:42.016 align:middle
And something we've
heard a lot is

14:42.016 --> 14:44.086 align:middle
that they could be a
disincentive to work.

14:44.736 --> 14:46.176 align:middle
Both of them addressed that.

14:46.176 --> 14:47.866 align:middle
I wonder if you would as well.

14:47.866 --> 14:50.596 align:middle
And then, as a follow-up, I
wonder what you've learned

14:50.596 --> 14:53.946 align:middle
about the degree to which
this has led to a widening

14:53.946 --> 14:56.146 align:middle
of the wealth gap
in this country,

14:56.296 --> 14:58.556 align:middle
the degree to which maybe people
are experiencing two different

14:58.556 --> 15:00.276 align:middle
kinds of-of pandemics here.

15:00.866 --> 15:03.636 align:middle
Some are getting through this
based on what they've saved

15:03.636 --> 15:04.896 align:middle
and the jobs that they have.

15:05.106 --> 15:06.946 align:middle
Others are really
struggling to get by.

15:06.946 --> 15:09.496 align:middle
And I wonder what role-what
additional role the Fed could

15:09.496 --> 15:11.556 align:middle
have in, sort of, bridging
that-that yawning gap.

15:13.366 --> 15:14.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:14.036 --> 15:18.326 align:middle
So, on your first
question, I wouldn't want

15:18.326 --> 15:21.366 align:middle
to be giving very
detailed, specific advice

15:21.406 --> 15:24.266 align:middle
on particular programs and
the level they should be at

15:24.266 --> 15:24.986 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

15:24.986 --> 15:27.606 align:middle
I just will say the
following, that, you know,

15:27.606 --> 15:31.776 align:middle
this-this pandemic and its
fallout really represents the

15:31.776 --> 15:34.706 align:middle
biggest shock to the U.S.
economy in living memory.

15:35.256 --> 15:37.176 align:middle
We went from the lowest levels
of unemployment we'd had

15:37.176 --> 15:40.316 align:middle
in 50 years to the highest
levels we've had in 90 years,

15:40.316 --> 15:42.156 align:middle
and we did it in the
space of two months.

15:42.896 --> 15:44.766 align:middle
And I would say that
the response

15:44.766 --> 15:48.446 align:middle
from the fiscal authorities
was strong, it was fast,

15:48.986 --> 15:52.096 align:middle
it was broad-and
appropriately so.

15:52.546 --> 15:54.046 align:middle
And I think we are
seeing the results

15:54.046 --> 15:56.216 align:middle
of the earlier strong
fiscal actions.

15:56.216 --> 15:57.896 align:middle
When you see the
spending that's happening,

15:57.896 --> 15:59.496 align:middle
when you see small
businesses staying

15:59.496 --> 16:02.816 align:middle
in business even though
the economy hasn't fully,

16:02.816 --> 16:05.766 align:middle
successfully, sustainably
reopened yet in many places,

16:06.106 --> 16:10.046 align:middle
you are seeing what happens
with that-that money.

16:10.046 --> 16:13.186 align:middle
And so, in-in a broad
sense, it's been well spent.

16:13.186 --> 16:14.466 align:middle
It's kept people in their homes.

16:14.466 --> 16:15.856 align:middle
It's kept businesses
in business.

16:15.956 --> 16:18.006 align:middle
And that's all a good thing.

16:18.556 --> 16:21.266 align:middle
I think, in the broad
scheme of things,

16:21.806 --> 16:26.546 align:middle
that there will be a need
both for more support from us

16:26.616 --> 16:28.606 align:middle
and from more fiscal policy.

16:29.086 --> 16:31.006 align:middle
Fiscal policy is up to Congress.

16:31.256 --> 16:34.516 align:middle
You see the ongoing
discussions that they're having.

16:34.516 --> 16:37.546 align:middle
And it suggests to me
that there's-you know,

16:37.546 --> 16:42.126 align:middle
that both sides, they're
wrangling over-over various

16:42.126 --> 16:44.586 align:middle
provisions but nonetheless
believe that there is a need

16:44.626 --> 16:48.086 align:middle
for some additional
fiscal support.

16:48.166 --> 16:49.736 align:middle
And the last thing I'll say is

16:50.606 --> 16:54.376 align:middle
that if the expansion-even
if-sorry.

16:54.376 --> 16:57.736 align:middle
Even if the reopening goes well
and many, many people go back

16:57.736 --> 17:00.866 align:middle
to work, it's still going
to take a fairly long time

17:00.866 --> 17:04.976 align:middle
for the parts of the economy
that are-that involve lots

17:04.976 --> 17:07.256 align:middle
of people getting together
in close proximity.

17:07.406 --> 17:10.076 align:middle
And that means that many of
the people who were laid off

17:10.226 --> 17:14.946 align:middle
from those industries-and that's
restaurants, you know, bars,

17:14.946 --> 17:17.106 align:middle
hotels, public entertainment,
all of those places,

17:17.106 --> 17:20.076 align:middle
travel and accommodation-many
of those people are going

17:20.076 --> 17:22.606 align:middle
to find it hard to go back-they
can't go back to their old job.

17:22.606 --> 17:24.056 align:middle
There won't be enough
jobs for them.

17:24.056 --> 17:26.606 align:middle
So I think those people
are going to need support.

17:26.766 --> 17:28.816 align:middle
I can't say what the
exact level should be.

17:28.816 --> 17:29.586 align:middle
It's not our role.

17:29.586 --> 17:32.396 align:middle
But they're going to need
support if they're to be able

17:32.396 --> 17:35.456 align:middle
to pay their bills, to
continue spending money,

17:35.456 --> 17:39.206 align:middle
to remain in their current
rental house or-or apartment

17:39.206 --> 17:40.586 align:middle
or a house if they own it.

17:40.946 --> 17:43.616 align:middle
So I think there will be a need.

17:43.616 --> 17:48.616 align:middle
In terms of inequality,
really-so I think it's fair

17:48.616 --> 17:51.986 align:middle
to say that the burdens of
the pandemic have fallen on,

17:52.636 --> 17:54.636 align:middle
heavily-they've fallen
on everyone,

17:54.636 --> 17:57.316 align:middle
but they've fallen very
heavily on people who work

17:57.316 --> 18:01.076 align:middle
in the service industries in
relatively low-paying jobs.

18:01.146 --> 18:05.916 align:middle
So there was a-a figure that
came out of some of our research

18:05.956 --> 18:09.076 align:middle
that was that if you make
$40,000 a year or less,

18:09.676 --> 18:13.176 align:middle
then 40 percent-you
had a 40 percent chance

18:13.176 --> 18:15.166 align:middle
of losing your job
in April and May.

18:15.166 --> 18:17.756 align:middle
So it's falling very
heavily on-on people

18:17.756 --> 18:20.686 align:middle
who have the least
financial wherewithal to,

18:20.686 --> 18:22.896 align:middle
you know, to bear that.

18:22.996 --> 18:26.066 align:middle
And that happens to
be heavily skewed

18:26.066 --> 18:27.366 align:middle
to minorities and to women.

18:27.366 --> 18:30.076 align:middle
So that's just what
the pandemic is doing.

18:30.076 --> 18:34.096 align:middle
You know, in terms of what
we're doing, what we're trying

18:34.096 --> 18:38.956 align:middle
to do is create an environment
in the financial markets

18:38.956 --> 18:42.656 align:middle
and in the economy where those
people have the best chance they

18:42.656 --> 18:46.006 align:middle
can have to go back to work to
their old job or to a new job.

18:46.436 --> 18:47.446 align:middle
That's really what we're doing.

18:47.446 --> 18:50.636 align:middle
Everything we do is
directed at that.

18:50.636 --> 18:55.016 align:middle
And, you know, the-I would
say-I would say one last thing

18:55.016 --> 18:55.736 align:middle
on inequality.

18:55.736 --> 18:59.346 align:middle
That is, inequality is a-is an
issue-has been a growing issue

18:59.346 --> 19:02.396 align:middle
in our country and in our
economy for four decades.

19:03.056 --> 19:05.456 align:middle
And you see it-it
has many faces.

19:05.456 --> 19:09.406 align:middle
You see it in the relative
flattening-out of-of incomes

19:09.406 --> 19:12.236 align:middle
for people in low
and-lower and middle incomes

19:12.236 --> 19:13.386 align:middle
as compared to those at the top.

19:13.586 --> 19:17.066 align:middle
You see it in low mobility
where people-where the chances

19:17.066 --> 19:19.026 align:middle
of moving up from the
bottom to the middle

19:19.026 --> 19:22.156 align:middle
or the top have declined
and are lower than they are

19:22.156 --> 19:24.156 align:middle
in other comparable
wealthy countries.

19:24.156 --> 19:27.366 align:middle
So it's a serious economic
problem for the United States,

19:27.366 --> 19:30.226 align:middle
but it's-it's got underlying
causes that are not related

19:30.226 --> 19:33.406 align:middle
to monetary policy or to our
response to the pandemic.

19:33.406 --> 19:35.726 align:middle
Again, four decades of
evidence suggests it's

19:35.726 --> 19:37.186 align:middle
about globalization.

19:37.186 --> 19:39.416 align:middle
It's about the flattening-out
of educational attainment

19:39.416 --> 19:44.176 align:middle
in the United States compared to
our other competitor countries.

19:44.296 --> 19:46.536 align:middle
It's about technology
advancing, too.

19:46.956 --> 19:48.986 align:middle
If you're on the wrong
side of those forces,

19:48.986 --> 19:51.576 align:middle
it's been-your income
has stagnated.

19:52.126 --> 19:54.276 align:middle
So it's-it's a critical,
critical problem

19:54.276 --> 19:56.976 align:middle
for our society, but one
that really falls mainly

19:56.976 --> 19:59.216 align:middle
to fiscal policy
and other policies.

19:59.816 --> 20:02.976 align:middle
Our part of it is to-is
to push as hard as we can

20:02.976 --> 20:05.896 align:middle
on our employment mandate
while keeping price stability.

20:06.406 --> 20:09.566 align:middle
We saw what happened to
people at-at the lower end

20:09.566 --> 20:11.966 align:middle
of the income spectrum
late in the last expansion.

20:12.516 --> 20:14.976 align:middle
It was the best labor market
in 50 years, they told us.

20:15.366 --> 20:18.696 align:middle
We saw that the biggest wage
increases were going to people

20:18.696 --> 20:20.486 align:middle
at the bottom end of
the-of the wage spectrum

20:20.486 --> 20:24.216 align:middle
for the last couple of years of
that 10 year, 8 month expansion.

20:24.636 --> 20:27.216 align:middle
So a tight labor market
is probably the best thing

20:27.216 --> 20:31.856 align:middle
that the Fed can foster to-to
go after that-that problem,

20:31.856 --> 20:32.976 align:middle
which is a-a serious one.

20:36.176 --> 20:36.706 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:36.706 --> 20:37.636 align:middle
And now, Nick Timiraos.

20:39.836 --> 20:41.826 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

20:42.206 --> 20:42.476 align:middle
Yeah. [Inaudible] Journal.

20:42.676 --> 20:45.606 align:middle
Chair Powell, you have described
your asset purchase objective

20:45.956 --> 20:47.306 align:middle
as stabilizing markets.

20:47.726 --> 20:49.536 align:middle
And with markets
having stabilized,

20:49.536 --> 20:52.306 align:middle
aren't the asset
purchases now doing more

20:52.306 --> 20:53.646 align:middle
than address market function

20:54.046 --> 20:56.886 align:middle
by supporting your
macroeconomic objectives?

20:57.006 --> 21:00.166 align:middle
The other question I have is,
what is your strategy going

21:00.166 --> 21:03.566 align:middle
to be with respect to
using asset purchases

21:03.566 --> 21:06.626 align:middle
to support your macroeconomic
objectives going forward?

21:06.956 --> 21:08.636 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

21:09.656 --> 21:13.276 align:middle
So you're right, the asset
purchases in-in their current

21:13.276 --> 21:17.706 align:middle
size really sprang from severe
dysfunction in the Treasury

21:17.706 --> 21:19.546 align:middle
and MBS markets at the beginning

21:19.546 --> 21:21.516 align:middle
of the market reaction
to the pandemic.

21:21.986 --> 21:23.556 align:middle
And thanks to those purchases,

21:23.556 --> 21:26.426 align:middle
we have substantially
restored-not fully,

21:26.426 --> 21:29.746 align:middle
but substantially restored
functioning markets.

21:29.746 --> 21:31.486 align:middle
This is absolutely critical.

21:31.486 --> 21:34.246 align:middle
That market is really
one of the-it's part

21:34.246 --> 21:36.806 align:middle
of the absolute bedrock of
the global financial markets,

21:36.806 --> 21:39.656 align:middle
and it's-it's essential
that it-that it work well,

21:39.656 --> 21:40.526 align:middle
and it is doing so now.

21:40.836 --> 21:45.126 align:middle
And we've always said, though,
that we understand, accept,

21:45.126 --> 21:48.986 align:middle
and are fine with the fact
that-that those purchases are

21:48.986 --> 21:51.456 align:middle
also fostering a more
accommodative stance

21:51.456 --> 21:53.106 align:middle
of monetary policy,
which would tend

21:53.106 --> 21:55.716 align:middle
to support macroeconomic
outcomes.

21:56.346 --> 21:59.456 align:middle
So it's doing both,
and-and, you know,

21:59.456 --> 22:01.416 align:middle
we've understood
that for some time.

22:01.896 --> 22:04.326 align:middle
It's not-the programs are
not structured exactly

22:04.396 --> 22:06.166 align:middle
like the-the QE programs were

22:06.166 --> 22:10.316 align:middle
in the last-in the last
financial-in the aftermath

22:10.316 --> 22:11.916 align:middle
of the last financial crisis.

22:12.226 --> 22:15.316 align:middle
Those were more focused on
buying longer-run securities.

22:16.076 --> 22:17.216 align:middle
The current purchases are all

22:17.216 --> 22:19.776 align:middle
across the-the maturity
spectrum.

22:19.776 --> 22:21.586 align:middle
Nonetheless, they are
supporting accommodative

22:21.586 --> 22:22.386 align:middle
financial conditions.

22:22.386 --> 22:24.126 align:middle
I think it's-it's clear
that that's the case.

22:24.816 --> 22:27.156 align:middle
In terms of our strategy,
you know,

22:27.156 --> 22:28.406 align:middle
that just remains to be seen.

22:28.406 --> 22:31.526 align:middle
We-as you know, we've
spent a lot of time

22:31.526 --> 22:35.046 align:middle
in meetings this year looking
at the tools that we have

22:35.046 --> 22:37.206 align:middle
to adjust our current
stance of policy.

22:37.636 --> 22:40.036 align:middle
We do feel that our current
monetary policy stance is the

22:40.036 --> 22:41.666 align:middle
appropriate one.

22:41.716 --> 22:44.466 align:middle
You know, we cut rates close
to zero right at the beginning.

22:44.956 --> 22:47.536 align:middle
We-we ramped up asset purchases,

22:47.536 --> 22:49.046 align:middle
and those-those have
really helped.

22:49.406 --> 22:51.826 align:middle
And we gave forward guidance
on-on both of those things,

22:51.826 --> 22:54.066 align:middle
which the markets
appear to understand,

22:54.066 --> 22:56.916 align:middle
and market pricing is
consistent with those.

22:56.916 --> 22:58.776 align:middle
So we think we're-we think

22:58.776 --> 23:01.326 align:middle
that our-our policy
stance is a good one.

23:01.376 --> 23:03.656 align:middle
We're, of course, prepared
to adjust that stance

23:03.656 --> 23:05.286 align:middle
as appropriate and
when we deem it

23:05.286 --> 23:07.776 align:middle
as appropriate to-to
better foster achievement

23:07.776 --> 23:09.426 align:middle
of our goals, of course.

23:09.896 --> 23:13.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:14.086 --> 23:14.556 align:middle
Okay. Jeanna.

23:14.556 --> 23:15.276 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

23:15.276 --> 23:15.646 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

23:16.596 --> 23:19.356 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

23:19.746 --> 23:22.676 align:middle
You talked to Larry Fink at
BlackRock in March, April,

23:22.676 --> 23:25.456 align:middle
and May, according to
your public calendars.

23:25.456 --> 23:26.966 align:middle
I was wondering if you
could tell us a little bit

23:26.966 --> 23:29.546 align:middle
about what you talked
about, and, if the topic

23:29.546 --> 23:32.446 align:middle
of those conversations was the
corporate credit facilities,

23:32.446 --> 23:33.886 align:middle
how did you handle
potential conflicts

23:33.886 --> 23:35.896 align:middle
of interest during
those conversations?

23:37.136 --> 23:37.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:37.506 --> 23:40.136 align:middle
Well, so-so BlackRock
is just our agent.

23:40.436 --> 23:43.236 align:middle
You know, we make the policy
decisions in conjunction

23:43.356 --> 23:47.806 align:middle
with our colleagues, and they
just execute our-our plans.

23:47.966 --> 23:50.556 align:middle
I actually don't remember
exactly what I would have been

23:50.556 --> 23:52.646 align:middle
talking with him about, but
he's a-you know, he's the head

23:52.646 --> 23:54.476 align:middle
of a major service provider.

23:54.476 --> 23:57.646 align:middle
He generally checks in to
find out whether we're okay

23:57.646 --> 24:00.406 align:middle
with the quality of the
service that-that BlackRock

24:00.406 --> 24:01.106 align:middle
is providing.

24:01.456 --> 24:04.346 align:middle
I don't have the daily
face-to-face interaction

24:04.346 --> 24:07.146 align:middle
with anybody else at BlackRock
or-or, as you can see,

24:07.146 --> 24:08.856 align:middle
three phone calls in the
course of a few months.

24:08.856 --> 24:10.406 align:middle
It wasn't very many.

24:10.406 --> 24:13.526 align:middle
So I think, you know,

24:13.976 --> 24:17.506 align:middle
their conflicts are
managed extremely carefully

24:17.506 --> 24:21.096 align:middle
in the contractual
arrangements we have with them.

24:21.096 --> 24:25.376 align:middle
And, again, I would have-I-I
can't recall exactly what those

24:25.376 --> 24:27.146 align:middle
conversations were, but they
were-they would have been

24:27.146 --> 24:28.966 align:middle
about what are you seeing
in the markets and things

24:28.966 --> 24:30.936 align:middle
like that too-you know,

24:31.766 --> 24:35.086 align:middle
generally exchanging
information.

24:35.086 --> 24:37.596 align:middle
And he's-he's typically trying

24:37.596 --> 24:41.256 align:middle
to make sure that-that we
are getting good service

24:41.256 --> 24:43.236 align:middle
from the company that
he founded and leads.

24:43.336 --> 24:46.846 align:middle
That's-I'd say that's his
main objective when we talk.

24:48.066 --> 24:48.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:48.926 --> 24:49.616 align:middle
Okay, thank you.

24:49.616 --> 24:50.996 align:middle
We'll go to Scott Horsley.

24:51.826 --> 24:54.046 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

24:54.366 --> 24:55.396 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

24:55.396 --> 24:56.516 align:middle
Scott Horsley from NPR.

24:57.096 --> 25:01.406 align:middle
I wonder if you can give us
an update on the coin shortage

25:01.406 --> 25:04.916 align:middle
that you talked to lawmakers
about last month and what,

25:04.916 --> 25:08.216 align:middle
if anything, that tells
us about the, sort of,

25:08.316 --> 25:12.426 align:middle
economic circulatory system.

25:13.976 --> 25:14.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:14.866 --> 25:19.026 align:middle
Yes. So-the situation with
coins is that the-the quantity

25:19.026 --> 25:24.886 align:middle
of coins is going up but was
adequate before the pandemic.

25:24.886 --> 25:27.066 align:middle
The problem is, the
circulation kind of stopped

25:27.656 --> 25:30.366 align:middle
because stores were
closed, banks were closed.

25:30.366 --> 25:33.626 align:middle
It just-customers
weren't spending,

25:33.626 --> 25:36.386 align:middle
they were-so the coins
stopped moving in the system.

25:36.996 --> 25:39.736 align:middle
So we've been working for-ever
since that began to happen,

25:39.846 --> 25:41.016 align:middle
we saw it happening right away.

25:41.016 --> 25:46.316 align:middle
We've been working to try to-try
to reverse that disruption

25:46.316 --> 25:48.816 align:middle
of the supply chain and
restore normal circulation

25:49.206 --> 25:50.006 align:middle
for our coins.

25:50.006 --> 25:51.346 align:middle
So we're working
with the U.S. Mint,

25:51.346 --> 25:53.766 align:middle
which is the issuing
authority, to address the issue.

25:54.006 --> 25:58.326 align:middle
Just last week, the Mint-you
may have seen-issued a statement

25:58.326 --> 26:01.386 align:middle
asking for the public's help
in keeping coins circulating,

26:01.386 --> 26:03.356 align:middle
and various people, you know,

26:03.356 --> 26:05.496 align:middle
went and put their coins
back into circulation.

26:06.056 --> 26:08.316 align:middle
We also created a coin
task force with all

26:08.316 --> 26:12.166 align:middle
of the stakeholders-you
know, banks and, you know,

26:12.166 --> 26:14.606 align:middle
the armored carriers,
the banking community,

26:14.696 --> 26:19.456 align:middle
credit unions, everybody in
the-in the coin supply chain.

26:19.996 --> 26:23.306 align:middle
We're in frequent
communication with the banks

26:23.306 --> 26:27.426 align:middle
and with the-with the armored
carriers trying to get back

26:27.426 --> 26:28.726 align:middle
to where we need to be.

26:29.276 --> 26:31.486 align:middle
So we do think the
inventories are building up.

26:31.706 --> 26:34.166 align:middle
You know, the Mint-the Mint
is making coins as fast

26:34.166 --> 26:35.896 align:middle
as they can, but-but, you know,

26:35.896 --> 26:38.276 align:middle
things happen with-in
the factory environment:

26:38.636 --> 26:39.596 align:middle
Someone will come to work

26:39.596 --> 26:42.366 align:middle
with COVID-I think this has
happened in-in almost every

26:42.366 --> 26:44.666 align:middle
factory environment around the
country-and then they'll shut

26:44.696 --> 26:46.166 align:middle
down for a day, and
then they come back,

26:46.166 --> 26:46.786 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

26:46.786 --> 26:49.586 align:middle
So-so we're closely
monitoring it.

26:49.586 --> 26:51.406 align:middle
It's-you know, it's
a significant issue.

26:51.406 --> 26:55.316 align:middle
We've-we've got a lot of
resources on it, and we do feel

26:55.316 --> 26:56.936 align:middle
like we're-we're
making progress.

26:57.366 --> 26:58.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:59.466 --> 27:00.486 align:middle
Okay, thank you.

27:00.486 --> 27:01.116 align:middle
Rachel Siegel.

27:01.116 --> 27:02.386 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

27:02.816 --> 27:04.126 align:middle
Hey, Chair Powell.

27:04.126 --> 27:05.346 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

27:05.386 --> 27:08.766 align:middle
I'm wondering if you can
be a bit more specific

27:08.766 --> 27:10.856 align:middle
about any risks that
you see related

27:10.856 --> 27:13.106 align:middle
to a double-dip recession
or signs

27:13.106 --> 27:16.426 align:middle
that the recovery was stalling,
or maybe begin to stalling,

27:16.746 --> 27:18.036 align:middle
compared to some of the signs

27:18.036 --> 27:19.346 align:middle
that we were seeing
earlier in the summer.

27:19.706 --> 27:20.426 align:middle
Thanks very much.

27:20.976 --> 27:22.176 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:22.176 --> 27:25.926 align:middle
Yeah. So maybe I'll talk more
broadly about the-the outlook

27:25.926 --> 27:30.666 align:middle
and include your-include
your question in it.

27:30.706 --> 27:34.976 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, economic
activity and employment picked

27:34.976 --> 27:39.406 align:middle
up beginning in May
and right through June

27:39.406 --> 27:43.706 align:middle
and remained well below their
levels because, I'd say,

27:43.866 --> 27:46.936 align:middle
job gains have reversed about
a third of the job losses

27:46.936 --> 27:51.236 align:middle
from March and April, and
consumer spending has reversed

27:51.236 --> 27:52.216 align:middle
about a half of the drop.

27:52.776 --> 27:55.346 align:middle
So-but, nonetheless, those
were-those were sooner

27:55.346 --> 27:56.556 align:middle
and stronger than we expected.

27:57.036 --> 28:01.116 align:middle
So-but what happened then was,
along with that positive data,

28:01.116 --> 28:03.906 align:middle
we got-we got the virus
increases starting in the middle

28:03.906 --> 28:07.536 align:middle
of June in lots and lots of
states around the country.

28:08.176 --> 28:12.656 align:middle
And that brings us to a new-a
new point here, which is,

28:12.656 --> 28:14.726 align:middle
we need to-in addition
to dealing

28:14.726 --> 28:16.696 align:middle
with the health crisis-we
have to remember,

28:16.696 --> 28:19.446 align:middle
this is really a health
crisis, and these are people

28:19.446 --> 28:24.126 align:middle
who are having the coronavirus,
and it's taking a terrible toll,

28:24.486 --> 28:27.216 align:middle
so we've got to deal with the
economic ramifications of that.

28:27.216 --> 28:31.006 align:middle
So what we're seeing is
that we monitor quite a lot

28:31.146 --> 28:33.656 align:middle
of what we-what we
think of as sort

28:33.656 --> 28:35.666 align:middle
of nonstandard, high-frequency
data.

28:35.956 --> 28:39.566 align:middle
That's become a very important
thing, even more important

28:39.566 --> 28:41.636 align:middle
than usual in the
work that we do.

28:41.636 --> 28:44.946 align:middle
And what that data show, on
balance, is that the pace

28:44.946 --> 28:48.406 align:middle
of the recovery looks
like it has slowed

28:49.006 --> 28:51.786 align:middle
since the cases began
that spike in June.

28:52.066 --> 28:54.616 align:middle
So some measures of consumer
spending based on credit card

28:54.616 --> 28:56.976 align:middle
and debit card data
have moved down.

28:57.056 --> 28:59.556 align:middle
Recent labor market
indicators point to a slowing

28:59.556 --> 29:02.426 align:middle
in job growth, particularly
among smaller businesses.

29:03.026 --> 29:05.036 align:middle
Hotel occupancy rates
have flattened out.

29:05.596 --> 29:08.376 align:middle
People aren't going out to
restaurants, bars, gas stations,

29:08.376 --> 29:10.986 align:middle
pharmacies, and beauty
salons as much.

29:10.986 --> 29:14.466 align:middle
Consumer surveys, by the way,
which had dropped very sharply

29:14.526 --> 29:16.196 align:middle
when the pandemic arrived
and then moved back

29:16.196 --> 29:18.986 align:middle
up sharply-they look like they
may be softening again now.

29:19.626 --> 29:21.206 align:middle
There are still some
areas of strength.

29:21.566 --> 29:25.246 align:middle
Housing and motor vehicle
sales have still been strong.

29:25.316 --> 29:29.956 align:middle
Nonetheless, on balance, it
looks like the data are pointing

29:29.956 --> 29:31.956 align:middle
to a slowing in the
pace of the recovery.

29:32.376 --> 29:36.976 align:middle
But I want to stress, it's-it's
too early to say both how large

29:36.976 --> 29:38.906 align:middle
that is and how sustained
it will be.

29:39.236 --> 29:42.666 align:middle
We just don't know yet, because
we have to wait for-to see the,

29:42.666 --> 29:46.676 align:middle
you know, the actual data on
spending and employment come in,

29:46.756 --> 29:49.556 align:middle
and-but this-this is what
we're seeing, and, of course,

29:49.556 --> 29:51.506 align:middle
we're monitoring
it very carefully.

29:51.936 --> 29:56.826 align:middle
And I'd be remiss in not
stressing this enough.

29:57.106 --> 30:01.356 align:middle
The path of the economy is going
to depend to a very high extent

30:01.806 --> 30:03.786 align:middle
on the course of the
virus and on the measures

30:03.786 --> 30:06.046 align:middle
that we take to keep
it in check.

30:06.186 --> 30:07.856 align:middle
That is just a very
fundamental fact

30:07.856 --> 30:09.966 align:middle
about our economy right now.

30:10.706 --> 30:12.196 align:middle
The two things are
not in conflict.

30:12.516 --> 30:15.416 align:middle
You know, social-distancing
measures and fast reopening

30:15.416 --> 30:16.816 align:middle
of the economy, they
actually go together.

30:16.976 --> 30:18.436 align:middle
They're not in competition
with each other.

30:19.206 --> 30:22.736 align:middle
So- MICHELLE SMITH.

30:23.296 --> 30:23.886 align:middle
Thank you.

30:23.996 --> 30:24.966 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

30:24.966 --> 30:26.276 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

30:26.576 --> 30:29.466 align:middle
Hi. Well, just to follow
up on that a bit-and thanks

30:29.466 --> 30:34.146 align:middle
for taking my question-you added
the sentence into the statement

30:34.146 --> 30:36.726 align:middle
about the course of
the economy depends

30:36.726 --> 30:37.666 align:middle
on the path of the virus.

30:37.666 --> 30:43.366 align:middle
Is this-do you feel
this idea, or this view,

30:43.366 --> 30:45.266 align:middle
is not widely enough understood?

30:45.266 --> 30:50.596 align:middle
Do you feel-you had warned back
in May that too early reopening

30:50.596 --> 30:52.946 align:middle
or an incautious reopening
might harm the economy.

30:52.946 --> 30:55.766 align:middle
Do you feel that message
was not heard, and that some

30:55.766 --> 30:58.036 align:middle
of these reopenings took
place perhaps sooner

30:58.036 --> 30:58.626 align:middle
than they should have?

30:59.416 --> 30:59.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:59.986 --> 31:03.416 align:middle
You know, I think we
feel that it-it might be,

31:03.416 --> 31:07.816 align:middle
the most central fact or the
most central driver of the path

31:07.816 --> 31:10.006 align:middle
of the economy right
now is the virus.

31:10.336 --> 31:11.866 align:middle
And you're seeing
that again now.

31:11.866 --> 31:13.826 align:middle
You saw that in the-during
the lockdown

31:14.536 --> 31:18.656 align:middle
when we got cases way down, and
you saw the economy reopening,

31:18.936 --> 31:21.506 align:middle
and you saw spending
go up and hiring go up.

31:22.326 --> 31:26.696 align:middle
And now that the-that the
cases have spiked again-again,

31:26.696 --> 31:29.806 align:middle
the early data, the
high-frequency data suggest

31:30.326 --> 31:35.876 align:middle
that there is a slower pace
of growth, at least for now.

31:35.876 --> 31:37.936 align:middle
We don't know how deep
or how long that will be.

31:37.936 --> 31:40.116 align:middle
So it's such an important
sentence.

31:40.116 --> 31:42.736 align:middle
We just decided that
it needed to be

31:42.736 --> 31:44.356 align:middle
in our-in our postmeeting
statement.

31:44.356 --> 31:45.386 align:middle
It's so fundamental.

31:46.036 --> 31:48.166 align:middle
And, you know, I think
we can't say it enough.

31:48.166 --> 31:50.816 align:middle
It's-it's really important.

31:50.816 --> 31:53.006 align:middle
We're-you know, you can
think of it as three stages.

31:53.006 --> 31:55.546 align:middle
There's the lockdown, and we
know what that looks like,

31:55.546 --> 31:59.576 align:middle
and we'll get the data, I guess,
later this week on GDP being

31:59.576 --> 32:02.146 align:middle
down very historically
large amounts.

32:02.146 --> 32:06.036 align:middle
Then there was the reopening,
and we would expect that many,

32:06.036 --> 32:07.396 align:middle
many people would
go back to work.

32:07.396 --> 32:10.426 align:middle
Those whose jobs could
be done without-you know,

32:10.426 --> 32:12.896 align:middle
without exposure to lots
of people in tight groups,

32:12.926 --> 32:14.616 align:middle
they should have been-they
should be able to go back

32:14.616 --> 32:17.236 align:middle
to work during that
phase fairly quickly.

32:17.236 --> 32:20.486 align:middle
But that would depend on
being able to keep the virus

32:20.486 --> 32:22.996 align:middle
under control, which will
depend on wearing masks

32:22.996 --> 32:24.396 align:middle
and other social-distancing
measures.

32:24.826 --> 32:26.416 align:middle
So that's where we are.

32:26.796 --> 32:28.186 align:middle
It's such an important factor.

32:28.186 --> 32:30.466 align:middle
It's-it's in-we're
all talking about it,

32:30.466 --> 32:32.346 align:middle
so we thought it really
had to be in the statement.

32:32.406 --> 32:32.926 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:36.036 --> 32:36.556 align:middle
Thank you.

32:36.556 --> 32:36.976 align:middle
Mike McKee.

32:36.976 --> 32:39.466 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

32:39.466 --> 32:43.916 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I'm wondering
what it is-you've talked a lot

32:43.916 --> 32:48.296 align:middle
about using all of your tools,
and Governor Brainard talked

32:48.296 --> 32:49.846 align:middle
about moving from support

32:49.846 --> 32:52.576 align:middle
to accommodation-what it
is you can actually do.

32:52.576 --> 32:54.336 align:middle
You've lowered rates to zero.

32:54.336 --> 32:55.756 align:middle
You've set up the
lending programs.

32:55.756 --> 32:58.166 align:middle
There hasn't been much take-up.

32:58.166 --> 33:00.616 align:middle
And the tools you talk about
are generally in service

33:00.616 --> 33:03.126 align:middle
of keeping interest rates
low, where they already are.

33:03.126 --> 33:05.566 align:middle
So unless you were to go
to negative interest rates,

33:05.776 --> 33:09.556 align:middle
I'm wondering what additional
accommodation the Fed can bring.

33:10.226 --> 33:13.746 align:middle
Or is it really up to the
fiscal authorities at this point

33:13.856 --> 33:19.246 align:middle
to rescue the economy, to-to
add additional help for it?

33:19.916 --> 33:20.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:20.286 --> 33:21.716 align:middle
Right. So you're right.

33:21.716 --> 33:24.436 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

33:24.436 --> 33:26.816 align:middle
to support the U.S. economy
at this difficult time,

33:27.506 --> 33:31.086 align:middle
and we will always remain
committed in that sense.

33:31.606 --> 33:34.836 align:middle
We feel like we have ways

33:34.836 --> 33:39.596 align:middle
to further support the economy
certainly through our credit

33:39.596 --> 33:43.116 align:middle
and liquidity facilities, which
are effectively unlimited.

33:43.116 --> 33:44.896 align:middle
We can adjust those programs.

33:45.456 --> 33:47.696 align:middle
We also can adjust
our forward guidance.

33:47.696 --> 33:49.186 align:middle
We can adjust our
asset purchases.

33:49.186 --> 33:50.646 align:middle
So there are things
that we can do.

33:50.646 --> 33:55.176 align:middle
We-we feel like we have
the ability to do more.

33:55.596 --> 33:59.146 align:middle
But I would not disagree
with the-with the importance

33:59.566 --> 34:01.746 align:middle
of fiscal policy-with
your statement

34:01.746 --> 34:03.236 align:middle
about the importance
of fiscal policy.

34:03.236 --> 34:07.446 align:middle
Fiscal policy can do-can address
things that we can't address.

34:07.446 --> 34:09.896 align:middle
If there are particular
groups that need help,

34:09.896 --> 34:12.986 align:middle
that need direct
monetary help-not a loan,

34:12.986 --> 34:17.866 align:middle
but an actual grant as the PPP
program showed-you can save a

34:17.866 --> 34:21.116 align:middle
lot of businesses and a lot
of jobs with those in a case

34:21.116 --> 34:24.876 align:middle
where lending a company money
might not be the right answer.

34:24.876 --> 34:27.316 align:middle
The company might not want
to take a loan out in order

34:27.316 --> 34:29.946 align:middle
to pay workers who can't work
because there's no business.

34:30.336 --> 34:32.206 align:middle
So lending is a particular tool,

34:32.206 --> 34:34.246 align:middle
and we're using it
very aggressively.

34:34.696 --> 34:37.606 align:middle
But fiscal policy is essential
here, and I would say, again,

34:37.896 --> 34:42.276 align:middle
Congress's action early in the
pandemic-historically large

34:42.396 --> 34:44.936 align:middle
by any standard around
the world, and certainly

34:44.936 --> 34:47.446 align:middle
by U.S. standards-it's
really helping now.

34:47.666 --> 34:48.506 align:middle
It's really helping.

34:48.506 --> 34:52.426 align:middle
It's going to stand up very well
to-to scrutiny down the years.

34:52.426 --> 34:56.586 align:middle
This Congress's very fast and
very open-handed response,

34:57.066 --> 34:58.626 align:middle
I think, has really helped.

34:58.796 --> 35:02.306 align:middle
And I think-I know
that, as I've said,

35:02.306 --> 35:05.166 align:middle
very likely more will be
needed from-from all of us,

35:05.726 --> 35:08.966 align:middle
and I see Congress negotiating
now over-over a new package,

35:08.966 --> 35:09.926 align:middle
and I think that's a good thing.

35:10.146 --> 35:10.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:16.796 --> 35:18.016 align:middle
Okay. Edward Lawrence.

35:18.016 --> 35:19.806 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

35:19.806 --> 35:21.736 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking the question.

35:22.156 --> 35:24.136 align:middle
So you talked about it
several times today:

35:24.136 --> 35:26.366 align:middle
The FOMC statement
directly says the path

35:26.366 --> 35:28.546 align:middle
of the economy is linked
to the path of the virus.

35:28.546 --> 35:31.436 align:middle
It looks like a vaccine
might come this year,

35:31.436 --> 35:34.576 align:middle
either in October or
towards the end of the year.

35:34.576 --> 35:36.106 align:middle
Has the Committee
talked about how

35:36.106 --> 35:37.836 align:middle
that might change Fed policies?

35:38.136 --> 35:41.176 align:middle
And, as a follow, do you see a
vaccine helping inflation get

35:41.176 --> 35:44.026 align:middle
back to where you want it to be?

35:44.846 --> 35:45.596 align:middle
Thank you.

35:46.116 --> 35:47.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:47.126 --> 35:50.836 align:middle
So we-of course, the
concept of vaccines comes

35:50.836 --> 35:51.666 align:middle
up in our discussions.

35:51.666 --> 35:52.356 align:middle
Of course it does.

35:52.406 --> 35:56.676 align:middle
But I have to say, you know,
we're-our job is not to plan

35:56.676 --> 35:57.956 align:middle
for the upside case, right?

35:58.336 --> 36:00.166 align:middle
The upside case-we've
got that covered.

36:00.586 --> 36:02.766 align:middle
Our job is to plan
for all-the full range

36:02.766 --> 36:04.296 align:middle
of things that could happen.

36:04.296 --> 36:07.666 align:middle
And so we're assuming-we're
going to continue to assume

36:08.436 --> 36:11.396 align:middle
that our facilities are needed,
that our policies are needed,

36:11.706 --> 36:14.706 align:middle
and that the public needs-you
know, needs the support

36:14.706 --> 36:19.366 align:middle
that we're giving the public
until-until shown otherwise.

36:19.916 --> 36:22.846 align:middle
There's great uncertainty
around the development

36:22.846 --> 36:24.326 align:middle
of therapeutics and vaccines.

36:24.446 --> 36:26.816 align:middle
All of us want them to
happen as soon as possible,

36:27.546 --> 36:29.496 align:middle
but we can't plan on that.

36:29.496 --> 36:32.026 align:middle
We've got to hope for the
worst-hope for the [laughs]-hope

36:32.026 --> 36:33.976 align:middle
for the best and plan for
the worst, I guess it goes.

36:35.966 --> 36:37.636 align:middle
In terms of inflation,
I don't know.

36:37.856 --> 36:42.096 align:middle
I mean, I-you know, I
think, fundamentally,

36:42.456 --> 36:44.216 align:middle
this is a disinflationary shock.

36:44.456 --> 36:46.126 align:middle
I know there-there is
a lot of discussion

36:46.126 --> 36:49.486 align:middle
about how this might lead
to inflation over time, but,

36:50.246 --> 36:53.486 align:middle
you know, we're-we're seeing
disinflationary pressures

36:53.486 --> 36:55.046 align:middle
around the world
going into this.

36:55.736 --> 36:57.576 align:middle
Now we see a big
shock to demand,

36:57.926 --> 37:00.986 align:middle
and we see core inflation
dropping to 1 percent.

37:01.676 --> 37:06.056 align:middle
And I do think for quite
some time we're going

37:06.056 --> 37:09.636 align:middle
to be struggling against
disinflationary pressures rather

37:09.636 --> 37:12.046 align:middle
than against inflationary
pressures.

37:15.756 --> 37:16.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:16.756 --> 37:17.026 align:middle
Thank you.

37:17.026 --> 37:18.266 align:middle
Ann Saphir.

37:18.346 --> 37:18.686 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

37:18.686 --> 37:21.286 align:middle
Hi. Yes, thanks for
taking my question.

37:21.286 --> 37:25.716 align:middle
So, I mean, as you monitor
the rising infections

37:25.716 --> 37:29.506 align:middle
and the slowing pace of the
recovery, could you speak to,

37:29.506 --> 37:33.456 align:middle
what would the trigger be
for doing further easing?

37:33.456 --> 37:37.036 align:middle
And on the flip side of that,
when would it be reasonable

37:37.036 --> 37:40.766 align:middle
to expect the Fed to
consider raising rates again?

37:41.206 --> 37:43.196 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

37:43.316 --> 37:45.046 align:middle
So, you know, as we've said,

37:45.046 --> 37:47.546 align:middle
we're carefully monitoring
the situation.

37:47.546 --> 37:50.866 align:middle
We moved very quickly and
very aggressively early,

37:51.596 --> 37:53.296 align:middle
and we've been monitoring
the situation.

37:53.296 --> 37:56.346 align:middle
And I think the markets
and-and the economy-you know,

37:56.346 --> 37:58.356 align:middle
I think our policy
is in a good place.

37:58.956 --> 38:02.046 align:middle
But we've looked at-at
ways of adapting our policy

38:02.046 --> 38:04.006 align:middle
as time goes by, and
we're ready to do

38:04.006 --> 38:05.016 align:middle
that when we think
it's appropriate.

38:05.016 --> 38:06.426 align:middle
I can't give you a
specific trigger.

38:06.796 --> 38:09.316 align:middle
It really just is when
we think it would help:

38:09.586 --> 38:12.216 align:middle
Would it help more than
what we're doing now

38:12.616 --> 38:15.776 align:middle
to foster maximum
employment and stable prices?

38:16.646 --> 38:18.686 align:middle
Sorry, your second question was?

38:19.236 --> 38:26.896 align:middle
[Inaudible] I can't hear you.

38:26.896 --> 38:28.076 align:middle
Say it again?

38:28.326 --> 38:29.506 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

38:29.786 --> 38:32.226 align:middle
Sorry-for when it would
be reasonable for the Fed

38:32.226 --> 38:33.766 align:middle
to consider raising-
CHAIR POWELL.

38:33.766 --> 38:34.126 align:middle
Oh. ANN SAPHIR.

38:34.126 --> 38:34.456 align:middle
-rates again.

38:34.456 --> 38:35.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:35.526 --> 38:38.386 align:middle
So as I-as I said
earlier, or a while back,

38:38.386 --> 38:40.436 align:middle
we're not even thinking
about-thinking

38:40.436 --> 38:41.566 align:middle
about raising rates.

38:42.136 --> 38:43.736 align:middle
We're-we're totally focused

38:44.676 --> 38:47.986 align:middle
on providing the economy the
support that it will need.

38:47.986 --> 38:50.186 align:middle
We think that the economy
will need highly accommodative

38:50.186 --> 38:52.856 align:middle
monetary policy and
the use of our tools

38:52.856 --> 38:54.066 align:middle
for an extended period.

38:54.666 --> 38:57.586 align:middle
And we're absolutely
committed to staying

38:57.586 --> 39:01.186 align:middle
in this until-until
we're very confident

39:01.856 --> 39:03.276 align:middle
that that is no longer needed.

39:03.676 --> 39:05.926 align:middle
So I wouldn't look for
us to be sending signals

39:05.926 --> 39:08.536 align:middle
about cutting back on
facilities or anything

39:08.536 --> 39:10.136 align:middle
like that for a very long time.

39:10.136 --> 39:14.276 align:middle
We're-we're in this until-until
we're well through it.

39:14.506 --> 39:17.386 align:middle
And I think this-you know,
I think the picture is,

39:17.906 --> 39:20.196 align:middle
you have the-you
have the lockdown,

39:20.196 --> 39:21.336 align:middle
then you have the reopening.

39:21.956 --> 39:26.136 align:middle
But there's probably going to be
a long tail where a large number

39:26.136 --> 39:28.026 align:middle
of people are struggling
to get back to work

39:28.026 --> 39:30.346 align:middle
because those-those
heavily affected areas

39:30.346 --> 39:33.496 align:middle
of the economy are going
to be challenged, really,

39:33.496 --> 39:38.286 align:middle
to employ the millions of
people who are now out of work.

39:38.286 --> 39:42.496 align:middle
I think 14 million people are
now out of work who were working

39:42.496 --> 39:43.786 align:middle
in February, something
like that.

39:43.786 --> 39:46.766 align:middle
So that's going to
take a while, I think,

39:46.766 --> 39:48.116 align:middle
in everyone's reckoning.

39:48.116 --> 39:51.616 align:middle
And I just-everyone should
know that we're going

39:51.616 --> 39:52.816 align:middle
to be there for all of that.

39:53.246 --> 39:55.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:55.846 --> 39:56.306 align:middle
Thank you.

39:56.306 --> 39:59.216 align:middle
Catarina with Bloomberg.

39:59.366 --> 40:00.526 align:middle
CATARINA SARAIVA.

40:00.726 --> 40:01.176 align:middle
Hi, Chair.

40:01.176 --> 40:02.536 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

40:02.726 --> 40:06.046 align:middle
You mentioned earlier that
the labor market we had

40:06.286 --> 40:09.296 align:middle
in February was the best
we've had in 50 years,

40:09.296 --> 40:12.456 align:middle
yet at that point the Black
unemployment rate was still

40:12.526 --> 40:14.846 align:middle
double that of whites.

40:14.846 --> 40:19.746 align:middle
So I wanted to ask you, you
know, we've seen some economists

40:19.746 --> 40:21.956 align:middle
and even the Biden
campaign call for the Fed

40:22.236 --> 40:24.116 align:middle
to more specifically
target the gap

40:24.206 --> 40:27.566 align:middle
between minority
unemployment rates.

40:27.696 --> 40:30.796 align:middle
Even if you don't get a
mandate on this from Congress,

40:30.886 --> 40:33.676 align:middle
is it something you would
consider more explicitly

40:33.676 --> 40:34.486 align:middle
going forward?

40:34.806 --> 40:36.706 align:middle
And how would you do that?

40:36.806 --> 40:38.766 align:middle
And then I also wanted
to ask you

40:38.766 --> 40:45.466 align:middle
about former economist Claudia
Sahm's writings from last night.

40:45.616 --> 40:48.476 align:middle
She gave a pretty
harrowing account of her time

40:48.846 --> 40:53.006 align:middle
at the Fed-described harassment
and professional gaslighting

40:53.706 --> 40:55.786 align:middle
that others have also spoken to.

40:56.326 --> 40:58.026 align:middle
Is this your Federal Reserve?

40:58.766 --> 40:59.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:59.576 --> 41:03.516 align:middle
Okay. So, first, on the
unemployment, you're right.

41:03.516 --> 41:06.826 align:middle
And we always point this
out in our-in our remarks.

41:07.486 --> 41:12.036 align:middle
While the aggregate unemployment
rate was 3½ percent,

41:12.786 --> 41:15.666 align:middle
every-every economy-every market

41:15.666 --> 41:17.926 align:middle
in every economy has
longer-run issues.

41:17.926 --> 41:20.956 align:middle
And one of ours is just
the disparate level

41:20.956 --> 41:24.616 align:middle
of unemployment between-between
Blacks and whites.

41:24.696 --> 41:26.426 align:middle
And it's generally sort of twice

41:26.426 --> 41:28.416 align:middle
as much-twice as
high for Blacks.

41:28.416 --> 41:32.296 align:middle
So even-but this was
the lowest, you know,

41:32.296 --> 41:34.906 align:middle
Black unemployment rate
in 50 years-or, actually,

41:34.906 --> 41:36.276 align:middle
since we started
keeping records,

41:36.426 --> 41:38.166 align:middle
which was less than
50 years ago.

41:38.856 --> 41:39.826 align:middle
So that's just a fact,

41:39.826 --> 41:42.506 align:middle
and it's-it's not a
good fact, you know.

41:43.026 --> 41:45.766 align:middle
So we have started,
though, in recent years

41:47.216 --> 41:54.546 align:middle
to focus considerable time and
attention on disparate levels

41:54.546 --> 41:56.036 align:middle
of unemployment-for example,

41:56.036 --> 41:59.576 align:middle
among-among different racial
groups and demographic groups.

41:59.906 --> 42:02.156 align:middle
We regularly discuss
those differences

42:02.206 --> 42:03.736 align:middle
in our FOMC discussions.

42:04.336 --> 42:07.576 align:middle
We call them out in
testimony and in speeches

42:07.656 --> 42:09.896 align:middle
and in our Monetary
Policy Report to Congress.

42:09.896 --> 42:14.016 align:middle
You'll see it everywhere, in
all the things that we do.

42:14.016 --> 42:15.036 align:middle
So what does that mean?

42:15.146 --> 42:18.676 align:middle
I think-you know, I
think what we learned is

42:18.676 --> 42:23.686 align:middle
that a tight labor market
really does a lot of good things

42:23.776 --> 42:27.216 align:middle
for minorities and
people at the lower end

42:27.216 --> 42:28.966 align:middle
of the income spectrum
generally.

42:29.726 --> 42:32.736 align:middle
But it-you know,
tight labor markets,

42:32.816 --> 42:37.576 align:middle
it took 8 years-it took sort of
the 8th and 9th and 10th years

42:37.576 --> 42:40.816 align:middle
of that expansion to
get to those benefits.

42:40.816 --> 42:42.356 align:middle
So we need more than that.

42:42.356 --> 42:44.126 align:middle
That's not a good
strategy, waiting 8, 9,

42:44.126 --> 42:45.126 align:middle
10 years to get there.

42:45.126 --> 42:47.846 align:middle
So we're-we're going to get
back there as fast as we can,

42:47.846 --> 42:49.006 align:middle
because that's what we can do,

42:49.006 --> 42:52.176 align:middle
but society more broadly
can affect these things.

42:52.386 --> 42:54.306 align:middle
You know, we don't
really have tools

42:54.306 --> 42:57.956 align:middle
that can address distributional,
disparate outcomes

42:57.956 --> 42:59.756 align:middle
as well as fiscal policy.

43:00.106 --> 43:02.746 align:middle
And just policy
generally-education,

43:03.126 --> 43:06.976 align:middle
health care, all those things
are much better at doing that.

43:06.976 --> 43:11.696 align:middle
In terms of-so we do-you
will see that-you will see

43:12.116 --> 43:15.326 align:middle
that discussed-the Black
unemployment rate discussed all

43:15.326 --> 43:16.456 align:middle
the time in what we do.

43:17.176 --> 43:19.786 align:middle
And I think we clearly
understand now

43:19.786 --> 43:22.666 align:middle
that it's something to weigh

43:22.856 --> 43:24.056 align:middle
when you get a tight
labor market.

43:24.056 --> 43:25.856 align:middle
It's something that you want
to be focused on and weigh.

43:25.856 --> 43:28.506 align:middle
So it is very much in
our thinking-again,

43:28.616 --> 43:30.856 align:middle
in a world where we don't
really have the best tools

43:30.856 --> 43:33.046 align:middle
to address that.

43:34.036 --> 43:36.266 align:middle
So, something that we're doing.

43:36.396 --> 43:44.276 align:middle
So I have not seen that-I did
not see that blog post yet,

43:44.276 --> 43:45.686 align:middle
although someone did
mention it to me.

43:45.686 --> 43:50.556 align:middle
We, of course, have been in
meetings until just before this.

43:50.556 --> 43:53.226 align:middle
So, but-and I will
take a look at it.

43:53.226 --> 43:53.886 align:middle
I will do that.

43:54.066 --> 43:57.926 align:middle
And, you know, I-without having
had a chance to think about it,

43:57.976 --> 44:00.626 align:middle
read it, understand it, I'll
just say a couple things.

44:00.716 --> 44:04.026 align:middle
First, I think it's
fair to acknowledge

44:04.516 --> 44:07.426 align:middle
that there's been a lot
of pain and injustice

44:07.426 --> 44:09.576 align:middle
and unfair treatment that
women have experienced

44:09.576 --> 44:12.626 align:middle
in the workplace-not
just among economists,

44:12.676 --> 44:14.656 align:middle
but among economists
and at the Fed.

44:14.816 --> 44:17.856 align:middle
That's been going
on for far too long.

44:18.366 --> 44:20.496 align:middle
And, you know, like
every other organization,

44:20.496 --> 44:23.166 align:middle
the Fed could have done more
and-and should have done more.

44:23.696 --> 44:26.276 align:middle
I would say, though,
that we-you know,

44:26.326 --> 44:34.046 align:middle
we have made it a very high
priority to have as diverse

44:34.046 --> 44:36.446 align:middle
and inclusive an
organization as we can.

44:37.436 --> 44:39.686 align:middle
And I think we've made a
lot of progress on that.

44:39.686 --> 44:41.546 align:middle
I think we want a place
where it's free to speak,

44:41.546 --> 44:44.726 align:middle
where views are welcome,
where people can disagree

44:44.726 --> 44:46.186 align:middle
but must do so respectfully.

44:46.866 --> 44:49.426 align:middle
I think a lot of
organizations are coming

44:49.426 --> 44:51.626 align:middle
around to understanding
the importance of that.

44:52.216 --> 44:56.486 align:middle
And I-my experience has been,
you know, from my career

44:56.486 --> 44:57.566 align:middle
in business too, is

44:57.566 --> 45:02.746 align:middle
that organizations-the really
successful organizations

45:02.746 --> 45:04.046 align:middle
in our society get this.

45:04.416 --> 45:07.166 align:middle
They do. They-mostly, they
get it that if you want

45:07.166 --> 45:09.656 align:middle
to attract the best people,
this is what you-you're going

45:09.656 --> 45:12.046 align:middle
to attract the best
people by having a diverse

45:12.046 --> 45:16.316 align:middle
and inclusive workforce,
so-and workplace.

45:17.186 --> 45:19.296 align:middle
So we've made diversity
a priority.

45:19.366 --> 45:22.036 align:middle
We actually-you know,
this has been a big issue

45:22.036 --> 45:23.346 align:middle
for the economics profession.

45:23.346 --> 45:27.726 align:middle
We had the-the two heads
of the AEA task force

45:27.726 --> 45:29.686 align:middle
on this-who happen to
be named Ben Bernanke

45:29.686 --> 45:34.296 align:middle
and Janet Yellen-come here, and
I cohosted a-an event with them,

45:34.296 --> 45:35.926 align:middle
I guess, last year
or the year before,

45:36.426 --> 45:39.696 align:middle
which had several
hundred-500 people or something

45:39.696 --> 45:41.886 align:middle
like that listening in
about-about these issues.

45:41.886 --> 45:43.786 align:middle
So we're doing a lot.

45:43.786 --> 45:44.756 align:middle
I'm sure we can do more.

45:44.756 --> 45:47.116 align:middle
We're doing a lot to-you know,

45:47.506 --> 45:52.066 align:middle
to foster a respectful
climate-particularly for women,

45:52.066 --> 45:53.996 align:middle
but for-for all people.

45:54.316 --> 45:56.256 align:middle
So it's-it's a very
high priority

45:56.586 --> 45:57.996 align:middle
for us as an organization.

45:58.156 --> 46:00.276 align:middle
I will look into-into

46:00.996 --> 46:06.896 align:middle
that and-when I-you
know, when this is over.

46:08.586 --> 46:09.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:09.226 --> 46:09.886 align:middle
Okay, thank you.

46:09.886 --> 46:11.976 align:middle
Don Lee. DON LEE.

46:12.306 --> 46:14.686 align:middle
Chair Powell, on
the labor market,

46:15.456 --> 46:18.056 align:middle
jobless claims rose
again recently,

46:18.226 --> 46:20.816 align:middle
and new hiring activity
has weakened.

46:21.366 --> 46:24.946 align:middle
I wonder, how big are the risks
of the unemployment rate rising

46:25.406 --> 46:27.276 align:middle
and job growth turning
negative this summer?

46:27.986 --> 46:29.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:29.796 --> 46:31.996 align:middle
So as I mentioned
earlier, you know,

46:31.996 --> 46:35.876 align:middle
we're watching this
high-frequency data.

46:36.026 --> 46:40.176 align:middle
And, of course, the official
labor market reports come

46:40.176 --> 46:45.326 align:middle
out once a month, and no one has
had a very good record lately

46:45.326 --> 46:46.566 align:middle
of predicting what
they're going to say.

46:46.566 --> 46:48.716 align:middle
They've been, in May and June,

46:48.876 --> 46:52.536 align:middle
stronger than anybody
expected, particularly in May.

46:52.536 --> 46:57.266 align:middle
So I would say, some humility
is appropriate here in terms

46:57.266 --> 46:59.476 align:middle
of thinking we understand
exactly where this is going.

46:59.796 --> 47:02.926 align:middle
I think all we can say today is
that there's-there's evidence

47:02.926 --> 47:06.236 align:middle
in the high-frequency data,
the surveys, and, you know,

47:06.236 --> 47:09.456 align:middle
you're tracking-you get
pictures of spending.

47:09.456 --> 47:11.536 align:middle
You get pictures of
people's movements.

47:12.036 --> 47:16.566 align:middle
And what we-it looks like
we're seeing a slowdown

47:16.566 --> 47:17.346 align:middle
in the rate of growth.

47:18.116 --> 47:20.846 align:middle
That's-and that might
be-it might be short lived,

47:20.846 --> 47:21.806 align:middle
it might not be, you know.

47:21.806 --> 47:25.166 align:middle
And it seems to be related
to-and the timing of it seems

47:25.166 --> 47:29.136 align:middle
to be related to the
spike in cases that began

47:29.136 --> 47:29.996 align:middle
in the middle of June.

47:30.906 --> 47:34.026 align:middle
Now, I really don't
want to get ahead

47:34.026 --> 47:35.426 align:middle
of where the data are on this.

47:35.976 --> 47:37.656 align:middle
So we're just going
to have to see.

47:37.656 --> 47:39.576 align:middle
We're going to have to-the
data over the next couple

47:39.576 --> 47:40.906 align:middle
of months will answer
that question.

47:41.766 --> 47:44.856 align:middle
And recent experience suggests
we-we need to wait and see.

47:44.856 --> 47:47.196 align:middle
In any case, there's clearly
a risk that we're going

47:47.196 --> 47:50.466 align:middle
to see a slowdown in
the rate of growth

47:50.466 --> 47:52.546 align:middle
in economic activity
and in hiring.

47:53.106 --> 47:54.906 align:middle
It might still be
at a robust level.

47:54.906 --> 47:57.406 align:middle
Honestly, we-we will
not know until we start

47:57.406 --> 47:58.626 align:middle
to see more data come in.

47:59.996 --> 48:00.546 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:01.856 --> 48:02.256 align:middle
Thank you.

48:02.256 --> 48:03.296 align:middle
Victoria Guida.

48:04.706 --> 48:05.676 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

48:05.676 --> 48:07.476 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

48:07.476 --> 48:08.776 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

48:08.776 --> 48:12.056 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
the 13(3) facilities.

48:12.246 --> 48:15.786 align:middle
You all have used your emergency
authority to buy assets

48:15.956 --> 48:19.316 align:middle
that you can't directly buy,
like corporate bonds, ETFs.

48:19.676 --> 48:21.956 align:middle
So I was just wondering,
what is the scope

48:22.256 --> 48:24.766 align:middle
of your authority under 13(3)?

48:24.766 --> 48:27.046 align:middle
What type of assets
are you allowed to buy?

48:27.046 --> 48:29.786 align:middle
Could you buy equities
through an SPV, for example?

48:30.746 --> 48:31.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:31.126 --> 48:32.486 align:middle
You know, we haven't-we
haven't looked at that,

48:32.486 --> 48:34.806 align:middle
and we have no-no intention.

48:34.806 --> 48:37.156 align:middle
We haven't done any work or
thought about buying equities.

48:37.686 --> 48:42.866 align:middle
But, you know, we-we're bound by
the-by the provisions of 13(3),

48:42.866 --> 48:46.716 align:middle
which requires that we make,
you know, facilities-programs

48:46.716 --> 48:49.626 align:middle
or facilities of broad
applicability, meaning we have

48:49.626 --> 48:53.346 align:middle
to have-it can't just be
focused on one entity.

48:53.716 --> 48:55.876 align:middle
You know, it has to be a
broad group of entities.

48:55.876 --> 49:00.296 align:middle
There's a lot in 13(3)
about-about the solvency

49:00.296 --> 49:01.056 align:middle
of the borrowers.

49:01.056 --> 49:02.946 align:middle
You know, remember, it
was rewritten or amended

49:03.416 --> 49:05.766 align:middle
after the financial crisis,
and it was written in a way

49:05.766 --> 49:09.146 align:middle
that was-that was to-meant to
make it challenging to bail

49:09.146 --> 49:10.836 align:middle
out large financial
institutions.

49:10.946 --> 49:13.996 align:middle
That's-there was a lot in there
to make sure that, you know,

49:13.996 --> 49:15.936 align:middle
that they were going to be
solvent and things like that.

49:15.936 --> 49:18.426 align:middle
So, you know, we have to
meet those requirements.

49:18.426 --> 49:21.226 align:middle
You know, we haven't
looked and said-tried

49:21.226 --> 49:22.566 align:middle
to say, "What can we buy?"

49:22.566 --> 49:24.416 align:middle
and, you know, "Let's
make a complete list."

49:24.766 --> 49:27.476 align:middle
You know, we felt that,
using these facilities,

49:27.616 --> 49:32.846 align:middle
that we could buy corporate
bonds and-and municipal bonds,

49:32.846 --> 49:33.076 align:middle
too.

49:34.596 --> 49:37.256 align:middle
So- VICTORIA GUIDA.

49:37.436 --> 49:40.446 align:middle
So I just-sorry, just to
follow up really quickly.

49:40.446 --> 49:43.056 align:middle
So is it-is it generally
supposed

49:43.056 --> 49:44.886 align:middle
to be primarily directed
at debt instruments,

49:44.886 --> 49:45.926 align:middle
since you talked
about borrowers?

49:45.926 --> 49:46.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:47.326 --> 49:52.026 align:middle
The statute doesn't
say that, but, yeah,

49:52.026 --> 49:53.446 align:middle
you could read the statute
that way if you want.

49:53.606 --> 49:57.306 align:middle
Honestly, we haven't tried
to push it to, you know,

49:57.306 --> 49:59.006 align:middle
what's the theoretical
limit of it.

49:59.006 --> 50:01.286 align:middle
I mean, I think, clearly,

50:02.716 --> 50:04.356 align:middle
it's-it's supposed
to-it's supposed

50:04.356 --> 50:06.696 align:middle
to replace lending, you know.

50:06.696 --> 50:08.006 align:middle
That's-that's really
what you're doing.

50:08.006 --> 50:10.946 align:middle
You're stepping in to
provide credit at times

50:10.946 --> 50:12.476 align:middle
when the market has
stopped functioning.

50:12.476 --> 50:16.536 align:middle
That's fundamentally what
you're doing with 13(3).

50:16.536 --> 50:20.456 align:middle
And-and so I think you've got
to-you've got to sort of work

50:20.456 --> 50:21.476 align:middle
within that framework.

50:21.866 --> 50:23.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

50:25.596 --> 50:26.006 align:middle
Thank you.

50:26.006 --> 50:26.676 align:middle
Greg Robb.

50:29.196 --> 50:29.526 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

50:29.526 --> 50:31.696 align:middle
Hi. Thanks for taking
my question.

50:31.696 --> 50:32.766 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

50:33.266 --> 50:34.556 align:middle
Can you hear me?

50:34.866 --> 50:37.766 align:middle
Okay. One of the things in
this crisis that's unusual is

50:37.766 --> 50:42.036 align:middle
that companies are
cutting wages and salaries,

50:42.166 --> 50:44.596 align:middle
and that's something, you
know, we haven't seen much.

50:44.906 --> 50:48.186 align:middle
And I was wondering what the
Fed is thinking about this.

50:48.616 --> 50:53.346 align:middle
It-it seems to indicate that
inflation could be more volatile

50:53.686 --> 50:56.436 align:middle
in coming-coming
months and coming years.

50:56.436 --> 50:57.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:57.686 --> 51:00.716 align:middle
Well, I-I think that the
main thing that tells you is

51:00.796 --> 51:04.856 align:middle
that the-the labor market has
a long way to go to recover.

51:05.206 --> 51:06.816 align:middle
You know, we had a
good labor market.

51:06.816 --> 51:07.766 align:middle
It wasn't perfect.

51:08.346 --> 51:09.676 align:middle
There were always issues.

51:09.676 --> 51:11.306 align:middle
There are always going to
be issues within an economy.

51:12.016 --> 51:15.816 align:middle
But the labor market really
has a long way to go now.

51:16.026 --> 51:22.496 align:middle
Even with two very strong months
of job creation-7½ million jobs

51:22.536 --> 51:24.756 align:middle
between the two months-we
still have 14 million

51:24.756 --> 51:25.506 align:middle
people unemployed.

51:26.126 --> 51:28.676 align:middle
So it's just-it's
a long way to go.

51:28.676 --> 51:32.226 align:middle
And as-and that leads to a
situation where there are

51:32.226 --> 51:33.726 align:middle
so many people looking for jobs,

51:33.726 --> 51:35.976 align:middle
and we have the economy
only partially reopened.

51:36.616 --> 51:39.886 align:middle
So there aren't, you
know-until the economy-or

51:39.886 --> 51:42.856 align:middle
as the economy fully reopens,
people can go back to work,

51:42.896 --> 51:45.216 align:middle
but-so it's a tough situation.

51:45.316 --> 51:47.516 align:middle
And I've read reports of that.

51:47.516 --> 51:51.666 align:middle
You know, of course, the
reported wages went up because

51:51.666 --> 51:52.986 align:middle
of the composition
effect-you know,

51:52.986 --> 51:55.946 align:middle
it's low-paid workers
didn't go back to work,

51:55.946 --> 51:58.626 align:middle
so-they were the ones that
got laid off-so average hourly

51:58.626 --> 51:59.296 align:middle
earnings went up.

51:59.356 --> 52:03.146 align:middle
But clearly, clearly the
pressure-there will not be much

52:03.146 --> 52:06.106 align:middle
upward pressure on
wages and compensation

52:06.596 --> 52:08.336 align:middle
at the aggregate
level here in a world

52:08.336 --> 52:10.976 align:middle
where there are just an awful
lot of people looking for work.

52:11.656 --> 52:16.206 align:middle
And the main-so it just
underscores again the-the

52:16.206 --> 52:18.966 align:middle
urgency of doing
everything we can

52:18.966 --> 52:23.886 align:middle
to restore the labor force
and-and to support those

52:23.886 --> 52:26.916 align:middle
who are-who want to
be in the labor force

52:26.976 --> 52:29.376 align:middle
but whose jobs have gone
away for some period of time

52:29.376 --> 52:33.706 align:middle
as they find other work due
to this natural disaster.

52:34.146 --> 52:36.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

52:37.686 --> 52:38.206 align:middle
Thank you.

52:38.206 --> 52:38.876 align:middle
Jean Yung.

52:40.736 --> 52:41.326 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

52:41.756 --> 52:43.576 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

52:43.576 --> 52:44.516 align:middle
Thank you very much.

52:44.836 --> 52:47.256 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
forward guidance.

52:47.796 --> 52:51.086 align:middle
Do you see merits to
tying your asset purchases

52:51.116 --> 52:52.866 align:middle
to economic outcomes?

52:53.286 --> 52:55.766 align:middle
And would you consider
both inflation

52:55.766 --> 53:00.446 align:middle
and labor market conditions
as part of those outcomes?

53:00.446 --> 53:01.876 align:middle
And specifically,
for the labor market,

53:02.236 --> 53:05.016 align:middle
what conditions would you
want to see before you think

53:05.016 --> 53:06.386 align:middle
about pulling back stimulus?

53:06.736 --> 53:07.536 align:middle
Thank you.

53:07.756 --> 53:08.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

53:08.046 --> 53:11.726 align:middle
So that's a great discussion
that we-we've talked about that

53:11.726 --> 53:14.296 align:middle
at past meetings, and I imagine
we will at future meetings

53:14.816 --> 53:18.036 align:middle
and haven't made any
decisions yet on that.

53:18.656 --> 53:20.786 align:middle
But, you know, you've
got a couple ways to go.

53:20.786 --> 53:24.096 align:middle
You can tie-you can
tie them to dates.

53:24.346 --> 53:28.966 align:middle
You can say we will-for a
specific period of time,

53:28.966 --> 53:30.496 align:middle
we will keep rates at X level.

53:30.766 --> 53:32.626 align:middle
Or you can say we'll
keep them there

53:32.626 --> 53:36.736 align:middle
until you achieve a
certain macroeconomic goal.

53:36.736 --> 53:38.106 align:middle
And it can either be inflation

53:38.106 --> 53:40.636 align:middle
or it could be an employment
goal, as you pointed out.

53:41.206 --> 53:44.046 align:middle
And I think that, you know,
there's attraction in all

53:44.046 --> 53:45.566 align:middle
of those depending
on the situation,

53:45.566 --> 53:47.866 align:middle
I think, for obvious reasons.

53:47.866 --> 53:50.096 align:middle
You can imagine situations
where, you know,

53:50.096 --> 53:50.846 align:middle
where you'd really want

53:50.846 --> 53:52.826 align:middle
to be targeting macroeconomic
outcomes.

53:52.826 --> 53:54.256 align:middle
It's also the case, though,

53:54.256 --> 53:58.206 align:middle
that sometimes-sometimes
date-based guidance works too.

53:58.566 --> 54:00.456 align:middle
I think it really is
very fact specific,

54:00.456 --> 54:02.026 align:middle
and it's not something
we've-you know,

54:02.596 --> 54:03.966 align:middle
we haven't made any
decisions on that,

54:03.966 --> 54:07.416 align:middle
so I wouldn't be standing
here telling you we're going

54:07.416 --> 54:10.696 align:middle
to go this way or that way,
you know, should the time come

54:10.696 --> 54:12.906 align:middle
for us to-to change
our forward guidance.

54:15.856 --> 54:16.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:16.126 --> 54:16.526 align:middle
Thank you.

54:16.526 --> 54:17.426 align:middle
Hannah. HANNAH LANG.

54:17.426 --> 54:18.836 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

54:18.936 --> 54:20.616 align:middle
Thanks for doing this today.

54:20.616 --> 54:24.996 align:middle
I wanted to ask, on
the stimulus bill

54:25.106 --> 54:27.936 align:middle
that Senate Republicans are
considering, they've said

54:27.936 --> 54:31.066 align:middle
that they're weighing whether
to revise the Collins Amendment

54:31.066 --> 54:33.906 align:middle
in order to ease the tier
1 leverage ratio for banks.

54:33.906 --> 54:37.436 align:middle
Are you concerned that doing
so will deplete bank capital,

54:37.436 --> 54:40.296 align:middle
given that the Fed's
stress tests showed that,

54:40.296 --> 54:43.346 align:middle
under the most severe
scenarios for economic recovery,

54:43.346 --> 54:46.596 align:middle
that several banks would
approach the regulatory minimum

54:46.776 --> 54:47.786 align:middle
capital requirements?

54:48.516 --> 54:49.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

54:49.866 --> 54:55.886 align:middle
So-okay. So I think what the
stress tests showed is that,

54:56.636 --> 55:00.416 align:middle
under the "regular way"
stress test, the banks passed.

55:00.976 --> 55:01.756 align:middle
But right in the middle

55:01.756 --> 55:04.346 align:middle
of the-of the stress
test came the pandemic.

55:04.346 --> 55:08.716 align:middle
And so we quickly dropped
in three other scenarios,

55:08.716 --> 55:12.046 align:middle
which were, you know,
really bad scenarios.

55:12.046 --> 55:14.886 align:middle
And many of the banks
passed, but some didn't.

55:14.886 --> 55:16.836 align:middle
So we-and that was
done very quickly.

55:17.436 --> 55:20.966 align:middle
So we pivoted, and
we used the provision

55:20.966 --> 55:24.576 align:middle
that says we can-that says
there's been a material change,

55:24.576 --> 55:27.366 align:middle
and we're going to ask them to
resubmit their capital plans.

55:27.936 --> 55:32.016 align:middle
We also stopped buybacks-share
buybacks and limited dividends.

55:32.546 --> 55:34.506 align:middle
So we're working
through the details

55:34.506 --> 55:35.526 align:middle
of how all of that will work.

55:35.526 --> 55:37.996 align:middle
I think we're going to-I
think we're going to send

55:37.996 --> 55:41.486 align:middle
out the-the stress
scenarios on September 30th.

55:41.486 --> 55:44.136 align:middle
So, I would say,
that's-you know,

55:44.136 --> 55:45.776 align:middle
that-that process
is working that way.

55:46.226 --> 55:49.256 align:middle
And-but you're making
a connection to it,

55:49.446 --> 55:51.006 align:middle
to the Collins Amendment fix.

55:51.006 --> 55:55.786 align:middle
So what's going on
there is that,

55:56.196 --> 55:59.806 align:middle
like many other supervisors and
regulators around the world,

56:00.246 --> 56:04.596 align:middle
what we found is that our-we
have found our banks are strong.

56:04.596 --> 56:06.756 align:middle
They're strongly capitalized,
with lots of liquidity.

56:07.506 --> 56:09.706 align:middle
And they've really been
a source of strength

56:09.936 --> 56:14.066 align:middle
in this-in this crisis so far.

56:14.066 --> 56:16.276 align:middle
And they've written off bad
loans and things like that.

56:16.276 --> 56:19.216 align:middle
They're still well capitalized
and strong and will be, I think,

56:19.216 --> 56:23.106 align:middle
a source of strength
in this situation.

56:23.626 --> 56:28.636 align:middle
So-but what-what we did
earlier was, we-what happens is,

56:28.636 --> 56:31.846 align:middle
the banks run up against
their leverage ratio,

56:31.926 --> 56:34.466 align:middle
which is a non-risk-sensitive
measure just of the amount

56:34.466 --> 56:35.766 align:middle
of assets on the balance sheet.

56:35.766 --> 56:39.446 align:middle
So people put cash in-on
deposit at the banks,

56:39.826 --> 56:42.666 align:middle
and they reached the limit of
how-how much they could grow

56:42.766 --> 56:45.416 align:middle
or made loans-you know,
they-companies drew

56:45.416 --> 56:46.966 align:middle
down loans and deposited cash.

56:47.636 --> 56:51.536 align:middle
So we gave some leverage
ratio relief earlier

56:51.766 --> 56:57.706 align:middle
by temporarily-it's temporary
relief-by eliminating,

56:57.946 --> 57:00.996 align:middle
temporarily, Treasuries
from the calculation

57:00.996 --> 57:02.946 align:middle
of the-of the leverage ratio.

57:03.096 --> 57:06.666 align:middle
This is a-this is another
additional thing to do.

57:06.946 --> 57:09.016 align:middle
If Congress chooses to
do this, we would want it

57:09.016 --> 57:10.586 align:middle
to be explicitly temporary.

57:10.976 --> 57:12.826 align:middle
We-in other words, this will
not be a permanent change

57:12.826 --> 57:13.856 align:middle
in capital standards.

57:14.636 --> 57:16.996 align:middle
We haven't decided to do it,
but it would give us the ability

57:16.996 --> 57:20.716 align:middle
to allow banks to grow their
balance sheet and, in doing so,

57:20.716 --> 57:22.146 align:middle
to serve their customers better.

57:22.516 --> 57:23.626 align:middle
And, you know, I
have to tell you,

57:23.626 --> 57:26.996 align:middle
if you look around the
world, bank regulators-many,

57:26.996 --> 57:29.076 align:middle
many bank regulators around
the world have given leverage

57:29.076 --> 57:30.146 align:middle
ratio relief.

57:30.896 --> 57:33.286 align:middle
You know, Swiss National
Bank, the Bank of Japan,

57:33.536 --> 57:37.296 align:middle
the European Commission did,
the Bank of Canada did, we did.

57:37.716 --> 57:41.486 align:middle
It's-it's not, it's
not-what it's doing is,

57:41.486 --> 57:44.076 align:middle
it's allowing them to grow
their balance sheet in a way

57:44.076 --> 57:45.356 align:middle
that serves their customers.

57:45.356 --> 57:46.556 align:middle
That-that's really
what it's doing.

57:46.556 --> 57:48.196 align:middle
And, again, I would want it

57:48.196 --> 57:50.746 align:middle
to be explicitly
temporary if-if we do do it.

57:50.806 --> 57:53.256 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

57:54.206 --> 57:54.756 align:middle
Okay, thank you.

57:54.756 --> 57:56.796 align:middle
We'll go to Brian Cheung
for the last question.

57:56.796 --> 57:57.426 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

57:57.566 --> 57:58.196 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

57:58.196 --> 57:58.646 align:middle
Thanks so much.

57:58.646 --> 57:59.826 align:middle
Brian Cheung with Yahoo Finance.

57:59.826 --> 58:03.356 align:middle
I wanted to ask just how the
framework review is working

58:03.356 --> 58:06.166 align:middle
in tandem with your discussion
on explicit forward guidance.

58:06.166 --> 58:09.616 align:middle
So is the idea that you're
trying to have some order

58:09.616 --> 58:11.376 align:middle
to that, that you would
announce the findings

58:11.676 --> 58:14.486 align:middle
of the Fed review before
then committing to some sort

58:14.486 --> 58:17.546 align:middle
of explicit forward guidance or
even yield curve control policy?

58:17.836 --> 58:19.066 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

58:19.296 --> 58:23.386 align:middle
You know, so I do
think that completion

58:23.386 --> 58:27.506 align:middle
of the review would-first
of all, it's something

58:27.506 --> 58:30.276 align:middle
that was a very high priority
for-we were really looking

58:30.276 --> 58:33.016 align:middle
forward to completing it,
probably, at the June meeting,

58:33.016 --> 58:34.466 align:middle
but it might have been
the meeting before that.

58:34.466 --> 58:36.706 align:middle
We were right on track,
and then we got distracted.

58:37.376 --> 58:39.636 align:middle
I-and we weren't expecting
the pandemic, of course.

58:39.636 --> 58:40.276 align:middle
Nobody was.

58:40.766 --> 58:42.876 align:middle
So the pandemic came in.

58:42.876 --> 58:45.376 align:middle
I think it is important to
go back and finish that,

58:45.376 --> 58:48.546 align:middle
and I do think that
will inform, you know,

58:48.586 --> 58:50.436 align:middle
everything we do going forward.

58:50.656 --> 58:54.226 align:middle
I would also tell you, though,
that, to-to a very large extent,

58:54.436 --> 58:56.766 align:middle
the-the changes we'll
make to the Statement

58:56.766 --> 58:57.576 align:middle
of [on] Longer-Run Goals

58:57.576 --> 59:01.566 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy are
really codifying the way we're

59:01.566 --> 59:04.826 align:middle
already acting with
our policies.

59:04.826 --> 59:07.336 align:middle
To a large extent, we're
already doing the things

59:07.336 --> 59:08.156 align:middle
that are-that are in there.

59:08.156 --> 59:11.216 align:middle
This is just a way
of-of acknowledging that

59:11.216 --> 59:12.456 align:middle
and putting them
in the document.

59:12.456 --> 59:14.566 align:middle
I can't tell you what the
exact timing of that will be,

59:15.016 --> 59:19.126 align:middle
but I do think that's a-that's a
sensible way to think about it.

59:19.966 --> 59:21.716 align:middle
Thanks very much.

59:21.876 --> 59:22.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

59:23.216 --> 59:24.396 align:middle
Thank you very much.

